('391b6b62-32cd-5ae5-9a54-9b9e82871574'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis/clitoris:', '["Motor to bulbospongiosus","Motor to external sphincter","Terminal branch of pudendal nerve","Motor to ischiocavernosus","Sensory to scrotum/labia"]'::jsonb, 'Terminal branch of pudendal nerve', 'The dorsal nerve is the terminal sensory branch of the pudendal nerve.'),
('78a44107-8562-55e3-943d-c70052f8d324'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The perineal artery supplies:', '["Deep perineal pouch only","Ischiorectal fossa","Superficial perineal pouch","Gluteal region","Thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial perineal pouch', 'Perineal artery (branch of internal pudendal) runs in the superficial perineal pouch.'),
('197a975b-9610-591c-8e67-be5bce60f1ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The artery of the bulb supplies:', '["Epididymis and its associated anatomical structures","Glans penis and its associated anatomical structures","Corpora cavernosa and its associated anatomical structures","Bulbospongiosus muscle and the posterior part of the corpus spongiosum","Testis and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Bulbospongiosus muscle and the posterior part of the corpus spongiosum', 'Artery of the bulb (branch of internal pudendal) supplies the bulbar urethra.'),
('22a68c96-0633-5700-a85e-1838e4c649a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The urethral artery supplies:', '["Glans penis and its associated anatomical structures","Corpora cavernosa and its associated anatomical structures","The corpus spongiosum and the penile urethra","Epididymis and its associated anatomical structures","Testis and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The corpus spongiosum and the penile urethra', 'Urethral artery (branch of internal pudendal) runs with the urethra.'),
('f5e51f72-62b8-5d42-b9fd-5d71514b4292'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The transverse perineal artery:', '["Runs transversely in the superficial perineal pouch (supplies the transverse perineal muscles and","Supplies the glans and its associated anatomical structures","Supplies the scrotum and its associated anatomical structures","Supplies the crus and its associated anatomical structures","Supplies the bulb and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Runs transversely in the superficial perineal pouch (supplies the transverse perineal muscles and', 'Transverse perineal artery anastomoses with its counterpart across the midline.'),
('77343510-28ef-5264-b85b-7618ec35467d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The external pudendal arteries (superficial and deep):', '["Branches of the internal pudendal","Branches of the femoral artery","Branches of the superior gluteal","Branches of the inferior epigastric","Branches of the obturator"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of the femoral artery', 'External pudendal arteries cross the inguinal ligament to reach the root of the penis.'),
('3cabfa46-c577-5c14-bd9e-360b6ae3d82d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The superficial external pudendal vein drains into:', '["Internal pudendal vein","Internal iliac vein","Azygos vein","Portal vein","Great saphenous vein"]'::jsonb, 'Great saphenous vein', 'The superficial external pudendal vein drains the skin of the penis and scrotum.'),
('87f035c1-8fcc-523b-ac06-e8ecf41a838a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The deep external pudendal vein drains into:', '["Portal vein","Internal pudendal vein","Internal iliac vein","Great saphenous vein","Femoral vein"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral vein', 'Deep external pudendal vein accompanies the deep external pudendal artery.'),
('d86f079c-ee3f-5858-8329-17b7514bafbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The superficial inguinal lymph nodes drain:', '["Lower limb, anterior abdominal wall (below umbilicus), buttocks, external genitalia (excluding te","Only external genitalia and its associated anatomical structures","Only perineum and its associated anatomical structures","Only lower limb and its associated anatomical structures","Only abdominal wall and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Lower limb, anterior abdominal wall (below umbilicus), buttocks, external genitalia (excluding te', 'Superficial inguinal nodes (superomedial and superolateral groups) are clinically palpable.'),
('f75a3323-6882-5000-84d8-30fb00a752e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The deep inguinal lymph nodes drain:', '["Only lower limb and its associated anatomical structures","Only perineum and its associated anatomical structures","Superficial inguinal nodes and deep structures of the lower limb","Only genitalia and its associated anatomical structures","Only superficial nodes and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial inguinal nodes and deep structures of the lower limb', 'Cloquet node (most superior deep inguinal node) is in the femoral canal.'),
('a794f5fb-40a3-5b4a-9e4b-b07f7fe4c2ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The external iliac lymph nodes drain:', '["Lower limb only and its associated anatomical structures","Pelvic organs and its associated anatomical structures","Back only and its associated anatomical structures","Perineum only and its associated anatomical structures","Deep inguinal nodes and the anterior abdominal wall (below umbilicus) into common iliac nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Deep inguinal nodes and the anterior abdominal wall (below umbilicus) into common iliac nodes', 'External iliac nodes (along external iliac vessels) are 8-10 in number.'),
('4559178a-29a8-5153-ba50-de242fa7ca72'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'In a surgical context, The common iliac lymph nodes drain:', '["Only internal iliac and its associated anatomical structures","External and internal iliac nodes into para-aortic (lumbar) nodes","Para-aortic nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Inguinal nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Only external iliac and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'External and internal iliac nodes into para-aortic (lumbar) nodes', 'Common iliac nodes (around common iliac vessels) are 4-6 in number.'),
('b64e87dc-f709-5000-82e8-51e85c5b096d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The para-aortic (lumbar) nodes drain:', '["Only adrenal glands and its associated anatomical structures","Only pelvis and its associated anatomical structures","Lower limb, pelvis, kidneys, adrenals, testis/ovary into cisterna chyli","Only lower limb and its associated anatomical structures","Only kidneys and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Lower limb, pelvis, kidneys, adrenals, testis/ovary into cisterna chyli', 'Para-aortic nodes (along aorta and IVC) are the main retroperitoneal nodes.'),
('f911ad87-79d4-5f57-b44e-593de6214f29'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'On diagnostic evaluation, The cisterna chyli receives lymph from:', '["Only lower limbs","Only intestinal trunk","Only lumbar trunks","Intestinal trunk and lumbar trunks","Only pelvis"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal trunk and lumbar trunks', 'Cisterna chyli is the dilated origin of the thoracic duct.'),
('5ecbde62-455a-51e4-9042-1c1affb43789'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The thoracic duct drains into:', '["Azygos vein","Left venous angle","IVC","Right venous angle","SVC"]'::jsonb, 'Left venous angle', 'Thoracic duct (45 cm) begins at cisterna chyli and ascends through aortic hiatus.'),
('8633ea39-dce9-599c-a706-d628a0807857'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'With respect to management, The right lymphatic duct drains:', '["Lower body only and its associated anatomical structures","Entire body and its associated anatomical structures","Left body only and its associated anatomical structures","Abdomen only and its associated anatomical structures","Right upper quadrant (right head, neck, arm, and chest) into right venous angle"]'::jsonb, 'Right upper quadrant (right head, neck, arm, and chest) into right venous angle', 'Right lymphatic duct (2 cm) drains into the right subclavian vein.'),
('0a4fdb60-555b-5428-8ca3-751e4605d3c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, The lymph drainage of the scrotum is to:', '["Internal iliac nodes","External iliac nodes","Deep inguinal nodes","Para-aortic nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial inguinal nodes', 'Scrotum drains to inguinal nodes (unlike testis, which drains to para-aortic).'),
('5a136645-0826-5d05-803a-7dc6f78e0d05'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the penis is to:', '["Para-aortic nodes","Internal iliac nodes","Superficial and deep inguinal nodes","Sacral nodes","Obturator nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial and deep inguinal nodes', 'Penile cancer metastasis first to inguinal nodes (sentinel node biopsy).'),
('9c99b44f-a948-53dc-8bfa-ad3857a5e44f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the clitoris is to:', '["Superficial inguinal nodes only","Obturator nodes","Deep inguinal nodes and internal iliac nodes","Para-aortic nodes","Sacral nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Deep inguinal nodes and internal iliac nodes', 'Dual lymphatic drainage: to inguinal and pelvic nodes.'),
('282785e7-0dfa-5bbd-b26b-7d4e9a4de6ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the vagina (upper 2/3):', '["Obturator nodes and its associated structures","Sacral nodes and its associated structures","External and internal iliac nodes","Para-aortic nodes and its associated structures","Inguinal nodes and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'External and internal iliac nodes', 'Lower 1/3 of vagina drains to inguinal nodes.'),
('ded4e12c-cc79-59e9-9e33-47df4e50bdc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the uterine fundus:', '["Only para-aortic and its associated anatomical structures","Para-aortic nodes (along ovarian vessels) and external/internal iliac nodes","Only sacral and its associated anatomical structures","Only iliac and its associated anatomical structures","Only inguinal and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Para-aortic nodes (along ovarian vessels) and external/internal iliac nodes', 'Dual lymphatic drainage of the fundus.'),
('7a68530a-8dc9-51c0-af8f-e177a583ed5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the uterine body:', '["Para-aortic nodes and its associated structures","Obturator nodes and its associated structures","Sacral nodes and its associated structures","External and internal iliac nodes","Inguinal nodes and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'External and internal iliac nodes', 'Uterine body lymphatics accompany the uterine vessels.'),
('db26632c-e264-5e4c-92cf-81fa37161201'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the rectum (upper 2/3):', '["Inferior rectal nodes -> inguinal nodes","Middle rectal nodes -> internal iliac nodes","Sacral nodes","Superior rectal nodes -> IMA nodes -> para-aortic nodes","Obturator nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Superior rectal nodes -> IMA nodes -> para-aortic nodes', 'Upper rectum follows superior rectal artery lymphatics to IMA nodes.'),
('8e0615ce-3224-584d-ac50-cd5d6b703ea2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the rectum (lower 1/3):', '["IMA nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Para-aortic nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Inguinal nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Middle and inferior rectal nodes -> internal iliac and sacral nodes","External iliac nodes and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Middle and inferior rectal nodes -> internal iliac and sacral nodes', 'Lower rectum drains laterally to internal iliac nodes.'),
('f1ae00e9-f47b-517c-b221-8d80f320c075'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the anal canal (above dentate line):', '["Inferior rectal nodes -> inguinal nodes","Sacral nodes","Superior rectal nodes -> IMA nodes -> para-aortic","External iliac nodes","Internal iliac nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Superior rectal nodes -> IMA nodes -> para-aortic', 'Above dentate: follows superior rectal lymphatics (portal system).'),
('54ab08f2-1448-5b1f-96c7-698b351d4688'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The lymph drainage of the anal canal (below dentate line):', '["Internal iliac nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Sacral nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Superior rectal nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Para-aortic nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Inferior rectal nodes -> superficial inguinal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior rectal nodes -> superficial inguinal nodes', 'Below dentate: somatic drainage to inguinal nodes (systemic).'),
('c5a376da-a0ca-50a2-b6f0-badec80ec51c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The autonomic nerves of the pelvis:', '["Only parasympathetic and its associated anatomical structures","Only pelvic splanchnic and its associated anatomical structures","Only sympathetic and its associated anatomical structures","Sympathetic (T10-L2, hypogastric nerves) and parasympathetic","Only hypogastric and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic (T10-L2, hypogastric nerves) and parasympathetic', 'Dual autonomic innervation coordinates pelvic organ function.'),
('31d60697-159b-562b-992a-dd088a2823ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The pelvic pain afferents travel with:', '["Somatic nerves","Sciatic nerve","Parasympathetic nerves","Sympathetic nerves","Pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic nerves', 'Referred pelvic pain may be felt in the lower back, suprapubic, or gluteal region.'),
('ebd68984-3d48-57ed-8667-04b3d488e7d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The nerve supply of the bladder (sensory):', '["Stretch receptors in bladder wall -> parasympathetic (S2-S4) and sympathetic","Only sympathetic and its associated anatomical structures","Only sciatic and its associated anatomical structures","Only pudendal and its associated anatomical structures","Only parasympathetic and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch receptors in bladder wall -> parasympathetic (S2-S4) and sympathetic', 'Bladder fullness sensation is via parasympathetic afferents (S2-S4).'),
('cce3b9bf-ed25-5f3c-ba50-bad5a4d883e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The nerve supply of the rectum:', '["Pudendal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Sciatic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Parasympathetic (S2-S4, pelvic splanchnic) for sensation of distension and defecation reflex","Sympathetic and its associated anatomical structures","Inferior rectal nerve and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (S2-S4, pelvic splanchnic) for sensation of distension and defecation reflex', 'Rectal distension sensation is via parasympathetic afferents (S2-S4).'),
('5af23979-d36c-5934-9672-873911c8620a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The nerve supply of the cervix:', '["Obturator nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Femoral nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Sciatic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Pudendal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Frankenhauser plexus (paracervical) -> hypogastric nerve (T10-L1) for uterine contractions and di"]'::jsonb, 'Frankenhauser plexus (paracervical) -> hypogastric nerve (T10-L1) for uterine contractions and di', 'Labor pain from cervical dilation is transmitted via T10-L1 sympathetic afferents.'),
('319b3ef9-889d-5ee6-85a8-fedf26baa783'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'Epidural anesthesia for childbirth:', '["Injection into paravertebral space and its associated anatomical structures","Injection into pudendal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Injection into spinal canal and its associated anatomical structures","Injection into sacral hiatus and its associated anatomical structures","Injection of local anesthetic into the epidural space (L3-L4 or L4-L5) to block T10-S4 nerve roots"]'::jsonb, 'Injection of local anesthetic into the epidural space (L3-L4 or L4-L5) to block T10-S4 nerve roots', 'Provides pain relief for labor (both uterine and perineal pain).'),
('b9ff2fbe-3b84-5005-9aa0-7be390b8cf12'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'Spinal (subarachnoid) anesthesia:', '["Injection into caudal canal and its associated anatomical structures","Injection into epidural space and its associated anatomical structures","Injection into nerve root and its associated anatomical structures","Injection of local anesthetic into the CSF (L3-L4 or L4-L5) for rapid, dense block of the lower body","Injection into muscle and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Injection of local anesthetic into the CSF (L3-L4 or L4-L5) for rapid, dense block of the lower body', 'Used for C-section, perineal, and lower extremity surgery.'),
('141c623e-4dcd-5904-bd8f-4b0e581bb580'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'Combined spinal-epidural (CSE):', '["Only caudal","Only local","Only epidural","Only spinal","Both spinal and epidural"]'::jsonb, 'Both spinal and epidural', 'Allows ambulation during early labor (low-dose spinal).'),
('c0c1cead-0e7f-5ca2-921b-d28f3b1186c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The sacral canal is entered for:', '["Spinal anesthesia","Nerve block","Epidural anesthesia","Caudal epidural anesthesia","Joint injection"]'::jsonb, 'Caudal epidural anesthesia', 'Caudal block is used for perineal, anorectal, and pediatric surgery.'),
('1068b79f-a6f2-5541-8a70-799f9a4599ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The sacral hiatus is bounded by:', '["The iliac crests and its associated anatomical structures","The coccyx and its associated anatomical structures","The sacral cornua (bilateral), the apex of the sacrum (superior), and the coccyx","The ischial spines and its associated anatomical structures","The sacral promontory and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral cornua (bilateral), the apex of the sacrum (superior), and the coccyx', 'The sacrococcygeal ligament covers the sacral hiatus.'),
('b688b7e7-4403-5c1e-960a-da26e40d6eef'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The sacrococcygeal ligament:', '["A ligament of the spine and its associated anatomical structures","A ligament of the hip and its associated anatomical structures","A ligament of the knee and its associated anatomical structures","A ligament of the pelvis and its associated anatomical structures","The fibrous membrane covering the sacral hiatus"]'::jsonb, 'The fibrous membrane covering the sacral hiatus', 'A ''pop'' is felt when the needle pierces this ligament during caudal block.'),
('16373702-6d02-5de7-8ab8-88b4298ed4c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'Complications of caudal block include:', '["Only hematoma and its associated anatomical structures","Only dural puncture and its associated anatomical structures","Only infection and its associated anatomical structures","Only intravascular injection and its associated anatomical structures","Intravascular injection (toxicity), dural puncture (total spinal), infection, hematoma"]'::jsonb, 'Intravascular injection (toxicity), dural puncture (total spinal), infection, hematoma', 'Caudal block requires larger volumes (20-30 mL) than lumbar epidural.'),
('746e9cb7-755d-50f3-b962-c355b53e2e5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The urogenital peritoneum in the male embryo:', '["Different from female and its associated anatomical structures","Peritoneum is absent and its associated anatomical structures","The peritoneum covering the pelvic organs in the male is as described (rectovesical pouch","Same as female and its associated anatomical structures","No peritoneum in male pelvis and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneum covering the pelvic organs in the male is as described (rectovesical pouch', 'Male pelvic peritoneum forms the rectovesical pouch (the lowest point).'),
('836d0fc9-6549-55f9-8df5-6e1044ce112a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The urogenital peritoneum in the female embryo:', '["Forms the vesicouterine and rectouterine pouches","Only one pouch and its associated anatomical structures","Same as male and its associated anatomical structures","No pouches and its associated anatomical structures","Three pouches and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Forms the vesicouterine and rectouterine pouches', 'Female pelvic peritoneum has two pouches (anterior and posterior).'),
('479fa8f7-81bd-5aff-9cb5-b33cf3ec36a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The mesonephric (Wolffian) duct remnants in males:', '["Form the uterus and its associated anatomical structures","Are absent and its associated anatomical structures","Appendix of epididymis, paradidymis, and the seminal vesicle/ejaculatory duct","Form the fallopian tubes and its associated anatomical structures","Form the vagina and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Appendix of epididymis, paradidymis, and the seminal vesicle/ejaculatory duct', 'Wolffian duct derivatives: epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, ejaculatory ducts.'),
('837b928d-fc50-5ce6-9e2e-c9412c7ed8bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct remnants in males:', '["Appendix of testis, prostatic utricle","Form the scrotum and its associated structures","Form the penis and its associated structures","Form the prostate and its associated structures","Are absent and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'Appendix of testis, prostatic utricle', 'Mullerian duct (regressed in males by AMH from Sertoli cells).'),
('518101f9-3e4c-57d2-b985-dd174e652fb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The mesonephric (Wolffian) duct remnants in females:', '["Form the vagina and its associated anatomical structures","Form the uterus and its associated anatomical structures","Form the fallopian tubes and its associated anatomical structures","Epoophoron, paroophoron, and Gartner duct cysts","Form the ovaries and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Epoophoron, paroophoron, and Gartner duct cysts', 'Wolffian duct (regressed in females) may form Gartner duct cysts.'),
('0dec15f8-616e-57dd-9c4e-dbe888dadb82'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct derivatives in females:', '["Fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, and the upper 2/3 of the vagina","Clitoris and its associated anatomical structures","Labia and its associated anatomical structures","Lower vagina and its associated anatomical structures","Ovaries and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, and the upper 2/3 of the vagina', 'Mullerian ducts fuse in the midline to form the uterus and upper vagina.'),
('ebe43381-ef6e-5aae-af66-8e0805d1466b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The urogenital sinus derivatives:', '["Lower 1/3 of vagina, vestibular glands, urethra, and vestibular bulbs","Uterus and its associated anatomical structures","Upper vagina and its associated anatomical structures","Ovaries and its associated anatomical structures","Fallopian tubes and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Lower 1/3 of vagina, vestibular glands, urethra, and vestibular bulbs', 'Urogenital sinus (endoderm) forms the lower vagina and the vestibule.'),
('45a2fd29-6935-5283-ab40-78ab1d23e3a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The indifferent external genitalia (before differentiation):', '["Only the labioscrotal swellings and its associated anatomical structures","Only the genital tubercle and its associated anatomical structures","The phallus and its associated anatomical structures","Genital tubercle, urogenital folds, labioscrotal swellings (all develop into male or female struc","Only the urogenital folds and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Genital tubercle, urogenital folds, labioscrotal swellings (all develop into male or female struc', 'Under the influence of DHT (male) or absence of androgens (female).'),
('1a7246db-4325-5135-bda1-f779893b5f5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The genital tubercle develops into:', '["The clitoris (female) or the penis","The vagina and its associated structures","The labia minora and its associated structures","The labia majora and its associated structures","The scrotum and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'The clitoris (female) or the penis', 'Genital tubercle (anterior) elongates to form the phallus (penis/clitoris).'),
('3f6a0c68-053c-5b6c-aa26-115b03913d71'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The urogenital folds develop into:', '["Vagina and its associated anatomical structures","Clitoris and its associated anatomical structures","Labia majora and its associated anatomical structures","Labia minora (female) or the shaft of the penis","Scrotum and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Labia minora (female) or the shaft of the penis', 'Urogenital folds fuse in males (forming the penile urethra) and remain separate in females (labia minora).'),
('7a0f3fd8-4598-50d4-9b97-9da6de7cf68a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The labioscrotal swellings develop into:', '["Vagina and its associated structures","Labia minora and its associated structures","Penis and its associated structures","Clitoris and its associated structures","Labia majora (female) or scrotum"]'::jsonb, 'Labia majora (female) or scrotum', 'Labioscrotal swellings fuse in males (scrotum) and remain separate in females (labia majora).'),
('33360d30-71b3-5ba0-a7b3-449c12f8b1bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'Testicular descent is mediated by:', '["Only intra-abdominal pressure and its associated anatomical structures","Androgens (testosterone), MIS (AMH), and intra-abdominal pressure","Only androgens and its associated anatomical structures","Estrogens and its associated anatomical structures","Only MIS and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Androgens (testosterone), MIS (AMH), and intra-abdominal pressure', 'Descent occurs in 2 phases: transabdominal (MIS) and inguinoscrotal (androgens).'),
('f1d0ff5b-f48b-5218-bd0e-51614ef15712'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'The gubernaculum testis guides:', '["The prostate and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","The ureter and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","The bladder and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","The kidney and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","The testis through the inguinal canal during descent (attached to the lower pole of the testis an"]'::jsonb, 'The testis through the inguinal canal during descent (attached to the lower pole of the testis an', 'Gubernaculum (fibromuscular cord) shortens during descent, pulling the testis into the scrotum.'),
('96ae87bb-f7ca-5f98-bda8-a2c780ca00b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'Undescended testis (cryptorchidism):', '["Only torsion and its associated anatomical structures","Only cancer and its associated anatomical structures","Increased risk of testicular cancer (seminoma), infertility, and torsion. Orchiopexy before 1 year.","Only infertility and its associated anatomical structures","No complications and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of testicular cancer (seminoma), infertility, and torsion. Orchiopexy before 1 year.', 'Orchiopexy (surgical fixation of testis in scrotum) ideally at 6-12 months.'),
('d453186e-a041-50f2-bff6-6f6d0cd4906b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Pelvis and Perineum', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, The processus vaginalis:', '["The epididymis and its associated anatomical structures","Peritoneal diverticulum through inguinal canal (normally obliterates after testicular descent","The vas deferens and its associated anatomical structures","The gubernaculum and its associated anatomical structures","The spermatic cord and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneal diverticulum through inguinal canal (normally obliterates after testicular descent', 'Incomplete obliteration causes congenital inguinal hernia and hydrocele.'),
('c98180e1-cea3-5624-b475-522ce2cb61f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The skull consists of:', '["32 bones","14 bones","28 bones","22 bones","8 bones"]'::jsonb, '22 bones', 'The skull (cranium) has 8 cranial bones (calvaria and base) and 14 facial bones.'),
('72dcaf56-822e-5a48-94b2-cf326b16f2c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cranial bones include:', '["Parietal, temporal, occipital only and its associated anatomical structures","Frontal, parietal, occipital only and its associated anatomical structures","Sphenoid, ethmoid, frontal only and its associated anatomical structures","Frontal, temporal, occipital only and its associated anatomical structures","Frontal, parietal (2), temporal (2), occipital, sphenoid, and ethmoid"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal, parietal (2), temporal (2), occipital, sphenoid, and ethmoid', 'The 8 cranial bones form the cranial cavity that contains the brain and its coverings.'),
('e9b6f042-f3d5-58ac-ac4b-af0d1a977fde'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The facial bones include:', '["Only the maxilla and mandible and its associated anatomical structures","Only the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","Nasal (2), maxilla (2), zygomatic (2), lacrimal (2), palatine (2), inferior turbinate (2), vomer","Only the nasal and zygomatic and its associated anatomical structures","Only the maxilla and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal (2), maxilla (2), zygomatic (2), lacrimal (2), palatine (2), inferior turbinate (2), vomer', 'The 14 facial bones form the framework of the face.'),
('d297cfba-28b5-55be-9e9b-51c11c7774be'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The anterior fontanelle closes by:', '["3-6 months","18-24 months of age","3-5 years","6-12 months","At birth"]'::jsonb, '18-24 months of age', 'The anterior fontanelle (bregma) closes at 18-24 months. The posterior fontanelle closes by 3-4 months.'),
('e5a8ee78-85ac-5c24-b45a-b6f9b7d24448'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pterion is:', '["A foramen and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","A sinus and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","The H-shaped suture region where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet (the th","A fontanelle and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","A bone and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'The H-shaped suture region where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet (the th', 'The pterion (at the temple) overlies the middle meningeal artery (risk of extradural hematoma).'),
('c057239e-f184-5ff0-a511-45405445d90e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The middle meningeal artery runs:', '["Through the foramen ovale","In the subdural space","In the subarachnoid space","Deep to the pterion","Through the foramen spinosum"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to the pterion', 'The middle meningeal artery (from the maxillary artery) enters the skull through the foramen spinosum.'),
('2db600e2-f48e-5b00-b3ca-63d258d12c79'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'An extradural (epidural) hematoma often results from:', '["Venous sinus thrombosis","Fracture of the pterion","Rupture of a cerebral vein","Rupture of an intracranial aneurysm","Cerebral contusion"]'::jsonb, 'Fracture of the pterion', 'An extradural hematoma (lens-shaped, does not cross sutures) is a neurosurgical emergency.'),
('bfbddd56-39ab-5400-b55d-ba8381d5cf33'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The circle of Willis is located at:', '["The occipital lobe","The temporal lobe","The cerebellum","The base of the brain","The vertex of the brain"]'::jsonb, 'The base of the brain', 'The circle of Willis (anastomotic ring) connects the internal carotid and vertebrobasilar systems.'),
('0ae78de3-4438-55c5-bcc9-f3dc8f285b27'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The internal carotid artery enters the skull through:', '["The foramen spinosum","The jugular foramen","The foramen magnum","The foramen ovale","The carotid canal"]'::jsonb, 'The carotid canal', 'The internal carotid artery (ICA) has a cervical, petrous, cavernous, and cerebral segment.'),
('00644290-f7e0-5f5d-b037-ceadf078526e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The vertebral arteries enter the skull through:', '["The jugular foramen","The carotid canal","The foramen spinosum","The foramen ovale","The foramen magnum"]'::jsonb, 'The foramen magnum', 'The vertebral arteries (from the subclavian) pass through the transverse foramina of C1-C6 and join to form the basilar artery.'),
('717a661a-79d6-5e6d-aa07-3f4cb7c9c292'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The basilar artery is formed by:', '["The union of the ICAs and its associated anatomical structures","The union of the vertebral and the ICA and its associated anatomical structures","The union of the two vertebral arteries at the ponto-medullary junction","The anterior cerebral arteries and its associated anatomical structures","The posterior cerebral arteries and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The union of the two vertebral arteries at the ponto-medullary junction', 'The basilar artery (the main artery of the pons) runs in the basilar groove and bifurcates into the posterior cerebral arteries.'),
('5c624ece-ee4f-5e61-bda1-40f33b2a6ef2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The optic canal transmits:', '["Optic nerve only","Optic nerve and the maxillary nerve","Optic nerve and CN III","Optic nerve (CN II) and the ophthalmic artery","Optic nerve and the ophthalmic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (CN II) and the ophthalmic artery', 'The optic canal (in the lesser wing of the sphenoid) connects the middle cranial fossa to the orbit.'),
('18a72337-4bab-5fd0-996c-566e884faf6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The superior orbital fissure transmits:', '["Only CN VI and its associated anatomical structures","Only CN II and its associated anatomical structures","CN III, CN IV, CN V1 (ophthalmic division of CN V), CN VI, and the ophthalmic veins","Only CN III and CN IV and its associated anatomical structures","Only CN V1 and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'CN III, CN IV, CN V1 (ophthalmic division of CN V), CN VI, and the ophthalmic veins', 'The superior orbital fissure (between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid) connects the cavernous sinus to the orbit.'),
('a0d3ca58-9914-5609-90c7-9bebff48867e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The inferior orbital fissure transmits:', '["The ophthalmic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","CN III and its associated anatomical structures","Optic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","CN IV and its associated anatomical structures","Maxillary nerve (CN V2), infraorbital vessels, and the zygomatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (CN V2), infraorbital vessels, and the zygomatic nerve', 'The inferior orbital fissure (between the maxilla and the greater wing of the sphenoid) connects the pterygopalatine fossa to the orbit.'),
('264bb5f1-c33d-5f9d-bcbf-cfd290982ff1'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The foramen rotundum transmits:', '["Ophthalmic nerve","Mandibular nerve","Vagus nerve","Facial nerve","Maxillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve', 'The foramen rotundum (in the greater wing of the sphenoid) opens into the pterygopalatine fossa.'),
('0b06ef83-f73d-5019-bb76-e048db573f41'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The foramen ovale transmits:', '["Mandibular nerve (CN V3), accessory meningeal artery, and the lesser petrosal nerve","Facial nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Maxillary nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Vagus nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Ophthalmic nerve and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular nerve (CN V3), accessory meningeal artery, and the lesser petrosal nerve', 'The foramen ovale (in the greater wing of the sphenoid) connects the middle cranial fossa to the infratemporal fossa.'),
('fc74eccb-d758-5b38-9cf7-abef7599a01a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The foramen spinosum transmits:', '["Accessory nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Mandibular nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Maxillary nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Middle meningeal artery and the meningeal branch of CN V3","Facial nerve and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meningeal artery and the meningeal branch of CN V3', 'The foramen spinosum (posterolateral to the foramen ovale) transmits the middle meningeal artery (a branch of the maxillary artery).'),
('d8f3b8c3-7364-56d3-b3f3-39c3a5d6576d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The foramen lacerum:', '["Transmits the hypoglossal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Transmits the ICA and its associated anatomical structures","An open foramen and its associated anatomical structures","A closed foramen at the base of the skull (covered by cartilage, not a true canal — the ICA passe","Transmits the jugular vein and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A closed foramen at the base of the skull (covered by cartilage, not a true canal — the ICA passe', 'The foramen lacerum (between the sphenoid, petrous temporal, and occipital bones) is filled with cartilage in life.'),
('3aa60992-1786-510e-a3fa-5efbe94c61c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The internal acoustic meatus transmits:', '["Only CN VII and its associated anatomical structures","Facial nerve (CN VII), vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII), and the labyrinthine artery","Only CN VIII and its associated anatomical structures","CN V, CN VII, and CN VIII and its associated anatomical structures","CN VII and CN VIII only and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII), vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII), and the labyrinthine artery', 'The internal acoustic meatus (in the petrous temporal bone) opens into the posterior cranial fossa.'),
('394cb718-ce1c-5791-829b-c96d419b9a72'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The jugular foramen transmits:', '["Only the internal jugular vein and its associated anatomical structures","CN IX, X, XI, and the carotid artery and its associated anatomical structures","Only CN XI and its associated anatomical structures","Glossopharyngeal (CN IX), vagus (CN X), accessory (CN XI) nerves, and the internal jugular vein","Only CN IX and CN X and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (CN IX), vagus (CN X), accessory (CN XI) nerves, and the internal jugular vein', 'The jugular foramen (between the petrous temporal and occipital bones) is the largest foramen of the skull base.'),
('73598dca-355d-5f64-842c-e6bb3a5f51ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The hypoglossal canal transmits:', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve","Accessory nerve","Internal jugular vein","Hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve', 'The hypoglossal canal (above the foramen magnum, in the occipital bone) transmits CN XII.'),
('405f6f82-c6a1-52f0-8b20-3be4e497dae1'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The foramen magnum transmits:', '["Only the medulla and its associated anatomical structures","The internal carotid artery and its associated anatomical structures","Medulla oblongata, vertebral arteries, spinal accessory nerves (CN XI), and the anterior and post","Only CN XI and its associated anatomical structures","Only the vertebral arteries and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata, vertebral arteries, spinal accessory nerves (CN XI), and the anterior and post', 'The foramen magnum (in the occipital bone) is the largest opening at the skull base.'),
('5cde449b-2f02-53df-a92f-5bcb2f2da11e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The stylomastoid foramen transmits:', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Accessory nerve","Vagus nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve', 'The stylomastoid foramen (between the styloid and mastoid processes) is where CN VII exits the skull.'),
('68b989f3-506e-53b7-8ae0-0ba895071f1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) passes through:', '["The foramen ovale and its associated anatomical structures","The jugular foramen and its associated anatomical structures","The foramen rotundum and its associated anatomical structures","The internal acoustic meatus, the facial canal, and the stylomastoid foramen","The hypoglossal canal and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The internal acoustic meatus, the facial canal, and the stylomastoid foramen', 'The facial nerve (motor, sensory, and parasympathetic) has a complicated intratemporal course (geniculate ganglion).'),
('6d56885e-ae42-5c17-8bcb-c6408081f53c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The parotid gland is located:', '["In the submandibular space and its associated anatomical structures","In the pterygopalatine fossa and its associated anatomical structures","In the buccal space and its associated anatomical structures","In the retromandibular fossa (anterior to the ear, between the ramus of the mandible and the mast","In the sublingual space and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'In the retromandibular fossa (anterior to the ear, between the ramus of the mandible and the mast', 'The parotid gland (the largest salivary gland) is enclosed by the parotid capsule (a tough extension of the deep cervical fascia).'),
('abe7263f-5e32-5bfb-bead-2d13ebfe8f35'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The parotid duct (Stensen duct):', '["Opens at the duct of Bartholin and its associated anatomical structures","Opens at the parotid papilla and its associated anatomical structures","Opens at the sublingual caruncle and its associated anatomical structures","Runs from the anterior border of the parotid, crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator","Opens at the submandibular papilla and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Runs from the anterior border of the parotid, crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator', 'The parotid duct (5 cm) is palpable when the masseter is clenched (crosses the masseter and enters the buccal cavity).'),
('2102aa48-7eb1-5962-b28c-f04497133c75'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The structures within the parotid gland:', '["Only the retromandibular vein and its associated anatomical structures","Facial nerve (superficial and deep divisions), retromandibular vein, external carotid artery","Only the facial nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Only the external carotid artery and its associated anatomical structures","Only the lymph nodes and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (superficial and deep divisions), retromandibular vein, external carotid artery', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) divides within the parotid into its five terminal branches (temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, cervical).'),
('b0534355-3df0-5853-ba80-1b1b06de9eb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The facial artery is a branch of:', '["The lingual artery","The maxillary artery","The external carotid artery","The superficial temporal artery","The internal carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 'The external carotid artery', 'The facial artery (from the external carotid) winds around the mandible at the anterior border of the masseter.'),
('ced1e3bb-7f72-5885-99fe-e140e2285451'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The superficial temporal artery is a terminal branch of:', '["The external carotid artery","The facial artery","The occipital artery","The internal carotid artery","The maxillary artery"]'::jsonb, 'The external carotid artery', 'The superficial temporal artery (palpable in front of the ear) is commonly used for temporal artery biopsy.'),
('78960870-280d-5bc4-8833-7e4e6ce1917f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The maxillary artery is a terminal branch of:', '["The internal carotid artery","The lingual artery","The facial artery","The external carotid artery","The superficial temporal artery"]'::jsonb, 'The external carotid artery', 'The maxillary artery (the largest branch of the external carotid) supplies the deep structures of the face.'),
('db7c2209-7842-5f17-a115-bdf50c8e48e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is:', '["A synovial joint between the mandibular condyle and the temporal bone","A fibrous joint and its associated anatomical structures","A cartilaginous joint and its associated anatomical structures","A hinge joint only and its associated anatomical structures","A ball-and-socket joint and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A synovial joint between the mandibular condyle and the temporal bone', 'The TMJ (ginglymoarthrodial joint) combines hinge and gliding movements. The articular disc divides the joint cavity into upper and lower compartments.'),
('c4be1828-72a5-520b-a48c-9e6d2b12753a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The muscles of mastication are:', '["Masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid","Masseter and temporalis only and its associated anatomical structures","Masseter and pterygoids only and its associated anatomical structures","Temporalis and pterygoids only and its associated anatomical structures","Medial and lateral pterygoid only and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid', 'The four muscles of mastication (CN V3, mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve) elevate, depress, protrude, and retrude the mandible.'),
('df1f1998-4b77-54e8-beda-25ca2e72cfdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lateral pterygoid muscle:', '["Closes the jaw","Retrudes the mandible","Moves the jaw laterally","Depresses and protrudes the mandible","Elevates the mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses and protrudes the mandible', 'The lateral pterygoid (superior and inferior heads) is vital for chewing. The inferior head protrudes and depresses the mandible.'),
('a5ef7985-e100-5061-ae17-fb789f536082'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The temporalis muscle:', '["Depresses the mandible","Moves the jaw laterally","Opens the jaw","Protrudes the mandible","Elevates and retrudes the mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates and retrudes the mandible', 'The temporalis (CN V3) is a powerful elevator of the mandible (palpable at the temple during clenching).'),
('3a66fdc0-24a1-560c-b75e-81aa181c8256'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The masseter muscle:', '["Retrudes the mandible","Moves the jaw laterally","Opens the jaw","Elevates and protrudes the mandible","Depresses the mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates and protrudes the mandible', 'The masseter (CN V3) runs from the zygomatic arch to the ramus of the mandible.'),
('f1634e51-a84d-57ec-87cf-7659c87f2923'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The medial pterygoid muscle:', '["Moves the jaw laterally","Opens the jaw","Retrudes the mandible","Depresses the mandible","Elevates and protrudes the mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates and protrudes the mandible', 'The medial pterygoid (CN V3) forms a sling with the masseter around the angle of the mandible.'),
('06352cea-d11c-5f7f-a11f-8aa59f1c1020'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the soft palate include:', '["Only the palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus and its associated anatomical structures","Only the tensor and levator and its associated anatomical structures","Tensor veli palatini, levator veli palatini, palatoglossus, palatopharyngeus, and musculus uvulae","All of the above except the tensor and its associated anatomical structures","Only the musculus uvulae and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Tensor veli palatini, levator veli palatini, palatoglossus, palatopharyngeus, and musculus uvulae', 'All soft palate muscles except the tensor veli palatini are innervated by the pharyngeal plexus (CN X). The tensor is innervated by CN V3.'),
('516f9930-563f-5d2f-b2a0-845045c716b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The tensor veli palatini:', '["Pulls the palate posteriorly and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the palate and its associated anatomical structures","Constricts the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","Depresses the palate and its associated anatomical structures","Tenses the soft palate and opens the pharyngotympanic (eustachian) tube"]'::jsonb, 'Tenses the soft palate and opens the pharyngotympanic (eustachian) tube', 'The tensor veli palatini (CN V3) is responsible for opening the auditory tube during swallowing and yawning.'),
('c77db386-0e18-537a-9b54-2f2d95851898'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The levator veli palatini:', '["Opens the eustachian tube","Elevates the soft palate","Depresses the palate","Constricts the pharynx","Tenses the palate"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the soft palate', 'The levator veli palatini (CN X) elevates the soft palate and prevents nasal regurgitation during swallowing.'),
('047e65e6-8f1b-51cb-85ac-3125fda53b1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The palatoglossus muscle:', '["Depresses the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Protrudes the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Retracts the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the posterior tongue and constricts the oropharyngeal isthmus (innervated by CN X via th","Opens the eustachian tube and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the posterior tongue and constricts the oropharyngeal isthmus (innervated by CN X via th', 'The palatoglossus (CN X) forms the anterior tonsillar pillar (palatoglossal arch).'),
('60552366-6a1e-5a5c-81ee-17bd86f58f6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The palatopharyngeus muscle:', '["Constricts the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing (forms the posterior tonsillar pillar","Depresses the palate and its associated anatomical structures","Tenses the palate and its associated anatomical structures","Opens the eustachian tube and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing (forms the posterior tonsillar pillar', 'The palatopharyngeus (CN X) forms the posterior tonsillar pillar (palatopharyngeal arch).'),
('1caf6379-943b-5531-b417-21aa9011c11c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The musculus uvulae:', '["Depresses the palate","Constricts the pharynx","Pulls the uvula upward and shortens it","Elevates the palate","Opens the eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Pulls the uvula upward and shortens it', 'The musculus uvulae (CN X) is a paired muscle that elevates the uvula (uvula deviation away from the lesion in CN X palsy).'),
('98f44b7a-0852-5a69-b423-85fe5d1a3bcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pharynx is divided into:', '["Nasopharynx (above the soft palate), oropharynx","The trachea and the esophagus","The oral cavity and the throat","The nose and the throat","The larynx and the pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Nasopharynx (above the soft palate), oropharynx', 'The pharynx (a fibromuscular tube, 12-14 cm) extends from the skull base to the cricoid cartilage (C6).'),
('a29eb748-e9bd-5d90-b1e2-55e404e263d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The nasopharynx contains:', '["The lingual tonsils and its associated anatomical structures","The palatine tonsils and its associated anatomical structures","The larynx and its associated anatomical structures","The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids), the tubal tonsils, the pharyngeal openings of the eustachian tu","The esophagus and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids), the tubal tonsils, the pharyngeal openings of the eustachian tu', 'The nasopharynx (above the soft palate) is the only part of the pharynx that is not collapsible (rigid walls).'),
('144fbba6-c0d4-54d1-927c-973932c60c4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The eustachian (pharyngotympanic) tube connects:', '["The nose to the throat","The throat to the ear","The sinuses to the nose","The ear to the external auditory canal","The nasopharynx to the middle ear"]'::jsonb, 'The nasopharynx to the middle ear', 'The eustachian tube (4 cm in adults, shorter and more horizontal in children) is opened by the tensor veli palatini during swallowing.'),
('d0c99c13-a1eb-5a3a-9306-37128af59fb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The oropharynx contains:', '["The larynx and its associated anatomical structures","The palatine tonsils, the base of the tongue, the soft palate, and the pharyngeal walls (includin","The esophagus and its associated anatomical structures","The adenoids and its associated anatomical structures","The trachea and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The palatine tonsils, the base of the tongue, the soft palate, and the pharyngeal walls (includin', 'The oropharynx (oral part) is separated from the nasopharynx by the soft palate and from the laryngopharynx by the epiglottis.'),
('1d523749-8ae7-5a58-8ec9-745fae5c4997'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The palatine tonsils are located:', '["In the tonsillar fossa","At the base of the tongue","In the nasopharynx","In the cheek","In the larynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the tonsillar fossa', 'The palatine tonsils (lymphoid tissue) are covered by a fibrous capsule (the peritonsillar space is the site of peritonsillar abscess/quinsy).'),
('0f7caf83-0a17-5e69-adf7-fa723ea9939a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The Waldeyer ring is:', '["A cartilaginous ring and its associated anatomical structures","A blood vessel ring and its associated anatomical structures","A nerve ring in the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","A muscle ring and its associated anatomical structures","An oropharyngeal lymphoid ring consisting of the pharyngeal, tubal, palatine, and lingual tonsils"]'::jsonb, 'An oropharyngeal lymphoid ring consisting of the pharyngeal, tubal, palatine, and lingual tonsils', 'Waldeyer ring (tonsillar ring) provides immunological surveillance at the entrance of the aerodigestive tract.'),
('bd1112e6-9f45-5583-9d56-61c285c4991e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The larynx is located at:', '["C3-C6","C5-C7","T1-T3","C2-C5","C1-C3"]'::jsonb, 'C3-C6', 'The larynx (voice box) is the sphincter that protects the lower airway and produces sound (phonation).'),
('588f1ed1-295e-5cca-925c-bb6b729a5ac2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The laryngeal cartilages include:', '["Only the cricoid and epiglottis and its associated anatomical structures","Only the thyroid and cricoid and its associated anatomical structures","Thyroid, cricoid, epiglottis, and the paired arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages","Only the epiglottis and arytenoids and its associated anatomical structures","The hyoid bone and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid, cricoid, epiglottis, and the paired arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages', 'The thyroid, cricoid, and most of the arytenoid cartilages are hyaline (may ossify). The epiglottis, corniculate, and cuneiform are elastic cartilage.'),
('f0048325-dc85-548b-a107-0a9407c15ef8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cricoid cartilage is:', '["A C-shaped cartilage and its associated anatomical structures","The smallest laryngeal cartilage and its associated anatomical structures","The only complete cartilaginous ring in the respiratory tract","The epiglottis and its associated anatomical structures","An incomplete ring and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The only complete cartilaginous ring in the respiratory tract', 'The cricoid cartilage (the narrowest part of the pediatric airway) is a complete ring (unlike the tracheal cartilages).'),
('b0e7b449-27ca-5aaf-b550-d30f4aff8935'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The vocal cords attach to:', '["The epiglottis and its associated anatomical structures","The cricoid cartilage and its associated anatomical structures","The thyroid cartilage (anterior commissure) and the arytenoid cartilages (vocal processes","The corniculate cartilages and its associated anatomical structures","The hyoid bone and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The thyroid cartilage (anterior commissure) and the arytenoid cartilages (vocal processes', 'The true vocal cords (vocal folds) are muscular folds containing the vocal ligament (elastic fibers).'),
('ef16fd08-5843-5c19-89d7-e8c7ffc6752d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The rima glottidis (glottic opening):', '["The pyriform sinus","The laryngeal inlet","The space between the true vocal cords","The vallecula","The space between the false vocal cords"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the true vocal cords', 'The rima glottidis (glottis) is the narrowest part of the adult airway (the cricoid is the narrowest in children).'),
('ab738ad8-3264-51e3-b519-1a9b0553971d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The false vocal cords (ventricular folds):', '["Above the true vocal cords","Below the true vocal cords","Same as the true vocal cords","Part of the cricoid","Part of the epiglottis"]'::jsonb, 'Above the true vocal cords', 'The false vocal cords (vestibular folds) act as a secondary sphincter to protect the airway.'),
('69b78f32-3db6-5cfe-87ba-1bb39fdd1122'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The laryngeal muscles include:', '["Only intrinsic muscles and its associated anatomical structures","Only the vocalis muscle and its associated anatomical structures","Only extrinsic muscles and its associated anatomical structures","Only the cricothyroid and its associated anatomical structures","Intrinsic muscles (adjust vocal cord tension and the glottic opening) and extrinsic muscles (elev"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic muscles (adjust vocal cord tension and the glottic opening) and extrinsic muscles (elev', 'The intrinsic laryngeal muscles (except the cricothyroid) are innervated by the recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X).'),
('39749954-e91c-5f13-bc8b-991cb59860c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cricothyroid muscle:', '["Adducts the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures","Abducts the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures","Opens the glottis and its associated anatomical structures","Relaxes the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures","Tenses the vocal cords (increases pitch — the only intrinsic muscle innervated by the external br"]'::jsonb, 'Tenses the vocal cords (increases pitch — the only intrinsic muscle innervated by the external br', 'The cricothyroid muscle (external laryngeal nerve, CN X) tilts the thyroid cartilage forward, increasing vocal cord tension.'),
('f16cc836-1432-5f3d-9d9b-82a7761f59b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The posterior cricoarytenoid muscle:', '["Adducts the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures","Relaxes the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures","Constricts the larynx and its associated anatomical structures","Tenses the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures","The only abductor of the vocal cords (opens the glottis — innervated by the recurrent laryngeal n"]'::jsonb, 'The only abductor of the vocal cords (opens the glottis — innervated by the recurrent laryngeal n', 'The posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA) is the primary muscle of respiration in the larynx (bilateral paralysis causes airway obstruction).'),
('f60f544f-2211-5e56-9d23-72ce51d5fffb'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve innervates:', '["All intrinsic muscles including the cricothyroid","Only the posterior cricoarytenoid","Only the cricothyroid","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid","Only the lateral cricoarytenoid"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X) hooks around the aortic arch on the left and the subclavian artery on the right.'),
('478fdcee-d43c-50be-ba76-f1555b1e7857'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The superior laryngeal nerve divides into:', '["Internal branch (sensory to the supraglottis) and external branch","Sensory to the subglottis and its associated anatomical structures","Motor to all laryngeal muscles and its associated anatomical structures","Sensory to the trachea and its associated anatomical structures","Motor to the vocalis and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Internal branch (sensory to the supraglottis) and external branch', 'The internal laryngeal nerve (superior laryngeal, CN X) pierces the thyrohyoid membrane and supplies the supraglottic mucosa.'),
('b23c2ee8-777f-5e98-81cf-77ffd3b8fb22'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The thyroid gland is located:', '["In the parotid region","In the submandibular region","In the posterior neck","Above the hyoid bone","In the midline of the neck"]'::jsonb, 'In the midline of the neck', 'The thyroid gland (2 lobes + isthmus) weighs 15-25 g in adults. The isthmus is at the level of the 2nd-4th tracheal rings.'),
('ebcd4357-30fd-5c42-aef1-43d7bc531638'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The thyroid isthmus is located at:', '["The sternal notch","The 5th-6th tracheal rings","The 2nd-4th tracheal rings","The cricoid cartilage","The hyoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'The 2nd-4th tracheal rings', 'The thyroid isthmus (palpable below the cricoid) connects the right and left thyroid lobes.'),
('8c31a3cb-408b-546c-ba8a-6480c28ac2b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pyramidal lobe of the thyroid:', '["A remnant of the thyroglossal duct","A pathological mass","A parathyroid gland","A blood vessel","A lymph node"]'::jsonb, 'A remnant of the thyroglossal duct', 'The pyramidal lobe (present in 50% of people) is a small, conical projection from the isthmus (the embryologic remnant of the thyroglossal duct).'),
('5d097b2d-2487-5cbb-9272-a7a7bd95e84d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The thyroid gland is supplied by:', '["Only the inferior thyroid and its associated anatomical structures","Only the superior thyroid and its associated anatomical structures","From the internal carotid and its associated anatomical structures","From the subclavian directly and its associated anatomical structures","Superior thyroid artery (from the external carotid), inferior thyroid artery (from the thyrocervi"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid artery (from the external carotid), inferior thyroid artery (from the thyrocervi', 'The thyroid is a highly vascular organ. The recurrent laryngeal nerve is closely related to the inferior thyroid artery.'),
('c6f5cde8-5302-591e-a5fd-2263e2f67496'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is related to:', '["The common carotid artery","The internal jugular vein","The inferior thyroid artery","The superior thyroid artery","The thyroid ima artery"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior thyroid artery', 'The relationship of the recurrent laryngeal nerve to the inferior thyroid artery is variable (a key concern in thyroid surgery).'),
('b1839cab-560f-55c9-9eab-314ce0e63fc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The parathyroid glands are usually:', '["6 glands","1 gland","8 glands","2 glands","4 glands"]'::jsonb, '4 glands', 'The parathyroid glands (3 x 5 mm) are located in the fascial plane between the thyroid capsule and the pretracheal fascia.'),
('e4b51d1b-1988-55ed-b52e-f8e0ccaaecf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The superior parathyroid glands are located:', '["Anterior to the thyroid","In the mediastinum","At the level of the cricoid cartilage","Below the inferior parathyroids","In the thymus"]'::jsonb, 'At the level of the cricoid cartilage', 'The superior parathyroids (from the 4th pharyngeal pouch) are relatively constant in location.'),
('2cde0194-7296-5129-aa4b-1bef3d071ab0'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The inferior parathyroid glands are located:', '["Above the superior parathyroids","In the maxilla","Near the lower pole of the thyroid lobe","In the submandibular region","In the parotid gland"]'::jsonb, 'Near the lower pole of the thyroid lobe', 'The inferior parathyroids (from the 3rd pharyngeal pouch with the thymus) have a more variable location.'),
('0912833e-1f1d-53aa-ac02-daf067ae4a34'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The hyoid bone is located at:', '["C1","T1","C7","The level of C3","C5"]'::jsonb, 'The level of C3', 'The hyoid bone (horseshoe-shaped) is suspended by the stylohyoid ligaments and the suprahyoid and infrahyoid muscles.'),
('5977f8d4-9b59-5daa-8ab7-ad16f83e2525'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The suprahyoid muscles include:', '["The strap muscles","Digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, and geniohyoid","Sternohyoid, thyrohyoid, omohyoid, and sternothyroid","Masseter and temporalis","The pharyngeal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, and geniohyoid', 'The suprahyoid muscles are in the floor of the mouth and elevate the hyoid bone (assist in swallowing and opening the jaw).'),
('fd947b3a-0167-5ee6-b2f0-105c12e4deb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The infrahyoid (strap) muscles include:', '["The muscles of mastication","Sternohyoid, thyrohyoid, omohyoid, and sternothyroid","The laryngeal muscles","Digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, and geniohyoid","The pharyngeal constrictors"]'::jsonb, 'Sternohyoid, thyrohyoid, omohyoid, and sternothyroid', 'The infrahyoid muscles (ansa cervicalis, C1-C3) depress the hyoid bone and the thyroid cartilage.'),
('fd9e4e05-5d68-5582-b4f6-160ea0e7f10a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The ansa cervicalis (ansa hypoglossi) supplies:', '["The laryngeal muscles and its associated anatomical structures","The suprahyoid muscles and its associated anatomical structures","The tongue muscles and its associated anatomical structures","The pharyngeal muscles and its associated anatomical structures","The infrahyoid muscles (sternohyoid, sternothyroid, omohyoid) — from C1-C3"]'::jsonb, 'The infrahyoid muscles (sternohyoid, sternothyroid, omohyoid) — from C1-C3', 'The ansa cervicalis (from the cervical plexus C1-C3) has a superior root (C1) from the hypoglossal nerve and an inferior root (C2-C3).'),
('79b800d0-2e81-5fa9-a74f-b99ad77ecca5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The carotid sheath contains:', '["Only the vagus nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Only the internal jugular vein and its associated anatomical structures","Common carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and vagus nerve","The sympathetic trunk and its associated anatomical structures","Only the carotid artery and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Common carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and vagus nerve', 'The carotid sheath (fascial layer) extends from the skull base to the root of the neck. The sympathetic trunk is posterior to the sheath.'),
('5758e744-2b13-57c9-a460-0235677528dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The common carotid artery divides into:', '["Medial and lateral branches and its associated anatomical structures","Right and left branches and its associated anatomical structures","Superior and inferior branches and its associated anatomical structures","Anterior and posterior branches and its associated anatomical structures","Internal and external carotid arteries at the carotid bifurcation (at the C4 level"]'::jsonb, 'Internal and external carotid arteries at the carotid bifurcation (at the C4 level', 'The carotid bifurcation (C4, at the level of the hyoid bone) contains the carotid sinus (baroreceptor) and the carotid body (chemoreceptor).'),
('1848da49-0805-523f-a302-a2e165471ee8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The carotid sinus is:', '["A dilation at the carotid bifurcation (contains baroreceptors that regulate blood pressure via CN","A chemoreceptor and its associated anatomical structures","The carotid body and its associated anatomical structures","The carotid space and its associated anatomical structures","A lymph node and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A dilation at the carotid bifurcation (contains baroreceptors that regulate blood pressure via CN', 'The carotid sinus (innervated by the carotid sinus nerve — a branch of CN IX) responds to stretch (increased BP induces bradycardia and vasodilation).'),
('1ba3b40e-2e65-5231-bacd-13acb9de3e78'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The carotid body is:', '["A chemoreceptor at the carotid bifurcation (sensitive to hypoxia, hypercapnia","A baroreceptor and its associated anatomical structures","A lymph node and its associated anatomical structures","The carotid sinus and its associated anatomical structures","The jugular foramen and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A chemoreceptor at the carotid bifurcation (sensitive to hypoxia, hypercapnia', 'The carotid body (1-2 mm) detects changes in arterial PO2, PCO2, and pH. Carotid body tumors (chemodectomas/paragangliomas) arise here.'),
('6325cec9-9579-51dc-8cc4-4d88167bcfad'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The external carotid artery branches include:', '["Only the maxillary and superficial temporal and its associated anatomical structures","Superior thyroid, lingual, facial, occipital, posterior auricular, ascending pharyngeal","Only the superior thyroid and lingual and its associated anatomical structures","All branches of the internal carotid and its associated anatomical structures","Only the facial and occipital and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid, lingual, facial, occipital, posterior auricular, ascending pharyngeal', 'The external carotid artery (ECA) supplies the face, scalp, neck, and upper pharynx.'),
('de5f082d-010f-5aea-9fe3-77a1eed49774'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The internal carotid artery has no branches in the neck:', '["It passes through the carotid canal, the cavernous sinus, and gives off ophthalmic","It gives off the facial artery and its associated anatomical structures","It gives off the maxillary artery and its associated anatomical structures","It gives off the superficial temporal artery and its associated anatomical structures","It gives off the lingual artery and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'It passes through the carotid canal, the cavernous sinus, and gives off ophthalmic', 'The internal carotid artery (ICA) has no branches in the neck (the first branch is the ophthalmic artery in the cranial cavity).'),
('8f94a145-fded-5999-a722-01620e3f77d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The internal jugular vein drains:', '["Only the face","Only the brain","The brain, face, and neck","Only the neck","The upper limb"]'::jsonb, 'The brain, face, and neck', 'The internal jugular vein (IJV) is the largest vein in the neck. The IJV runs in the carotid sheath with the carotid artery and the vagus nerve.'),
('e5792adb-5284-556b-ae51-f232d80afe6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The external jugular vein drains:', '["The deep neck structures","The brain","The upper limb","The scalp and the face","The thorax"]'::jsonb, 'The scalp and the face', 'The external jugular vein (EJV) is formed by the retromandibular and posterior auricular veins (visible when the jugular pressure is elevated).'),
('48a0c74c-f4c6-5b80-af45-47d8750e5ef7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cervical plexus is formed by:', '["The ventral rami of C1-C4","The ventral rami of C5-C8","The brachial plexus","The lumbar plexus","The dorsal rami of C1-C4"]'::jsonb, 'The ventral rami of C1-C4', 'The cervical plexus has cutaneous branches (greater auricular, lesser occipital, great auricular, transverse cervical, and supraclavicular) and muscular branches.'),
('e01171b3-da8d-523d-8c7c-362b6558f9f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The phrenic nerve (C3-C5) arises from:', '["The vagus nerve","The facial nerve","The lumbar plexus","The brachial plexus","The cervical plexus"]'::jsonb, 'The cervical plexus', 'The phrenic nerve (C3-C5 — C4 dominates) is the sole motor nerve to the diaphragm and provides sensory innervation to the pericardium.'),
('b26874e1-04c3-5362-ae30-fa4a68613e3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle:', '["Flexes the neck only","Rotates the head to the opposite side and flexes the neck","A lateral flexor only","Rotates the head to the same side","Extends the neck"]'::jsonb, 'Rotates the head to the opposite side and flexes the neck', 'The SCM (two heads: sternal and clavicular) is a key landmark in the neck (the anterior border overlays the carotid sheath).'),
('22c786b9-a2b2-5b8c-aeee-27c310847834'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The trapezius muscle:', '["Extends the neck and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates, retracts, and rotates the scapula","Protracts the scapula and its associated anatomical structures","Flexes the neck and its associated anatomical structures","Depresses the scapula and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates, retracts, and rotates the scapula', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) innervates the SCM and the trapezius (CN XI palsy causes shoulder droop and winged scapula).'),
('0ba63d7e-0daf-5289-8212-2f7ea0f1da64'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The posterior triangle of the neck:', '["Bounded by the mandible, SCM, and the midline","Bounded by the SCM (anterior), trapezius (posterior), and the clavicle","The muscular triangle","The carotid triangle","The submandibular triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Bounded by the SCM (anterior), trapezius (posterior), and the clavicle', 'The posterior triangle contains the external jugular vein, cervical plexus, phrenic nerve, and the brachial plexus (roots/trunks).'),
('b561ccee-89e0-508c-b189-988d2db08a01'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The anterior triangle of the neck:', '["Bounded by the mandible (superior), SCM (posterior), and the midline","The carotid triangle","Bounded by the SCM, trapezius, and clavicle","The muscular triangle","The posterior triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Bounded by the mandible (superior), SCM (posterior), and the midline', 'The anterior triangle is subdivided into the carotid, submandibular, submental, and muscular triangles.'),
('ed6fcbd4-c6ce-5956-80a9-5dc003f619c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The carotid triangle contains:', '["The carotid bifurcation, the internal and external carotid arteries, the internal jugular vein","The brachial plexus and its associated anatomical structures","The parotid gland and its associated anatomical structures","The phrenic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The thoracic duct and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The carotid bifurcation, the internal and external carotid arteries, the internal jugular vein', 'The carotid triangle (bounded by the SCM, omohyoid, and digastric) is the main surgical approach to the carotid arteries.'),
('beb78cbf-740a-51c2-b32b-b40619101efa'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The submandibular triangle (digastric triangle):', '["The muscular triangle and its associated anatomical structures","The posterior triangle and its associated anatomical structures","The carotid triangle and its associated anatomical structures","Bounded by the mandible and the anterior and posterior bellies of the digastric (contains the sub","The submental triangle and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Bounded by the mandible and the anterior and posterior bellies of the digastric (contains the sub', 'The submandibular triangle (floor: mylohyoid and hyoglossus) contains the submandibular gland, lymph nodes, and the facial artery and vein.'),
('20bcce3b-d7ab-57b5-b55e-eee2a7b668c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The submandibular gland is located:', '["In the sublingual space","In the parotid region","In the submental triangle","In the submandibular triangle","In the buccal space"]'::jsonb, 'In the submandibular triangle', 'The submandibular gland (the second largest salivary gland) has a superficial part and a deep part around the mylohyoid.'),
('abfeb533-d910-571a-98f9-2c449d473f26'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The submandibular duct (Wharton duct):', '["Opens at the sublingual fold and its associated anatomical structures","Runs from the submandibular gland to the sublingual caruncle (on each side of the frenulum of the","Opens opposite the upper second molar and its associated anatomical structures","Opens at the parotid papilla and its associated anatomical structures","Opens at the palatine tonsil and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Runs from the submandibular gland to the sublingual caruncle (on each side of the frenulum of the', 'The Wharton duct (5 cm) is crossed by the lingual nerve laterally (the duct is opened to remove a sialolith/calculus).'),
('620a8041-af14-5b78-b5b8-48bf3bc0847d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sublingual gland is located:', '["In the submandibular triangle","In the buccal space","In the parotid region","In the submental triangle","In the floor of the mouth"]'::jsonb, 'In the floor of the mouth', 'The sublingual gland (the smallest major salivary gland) has 10-20 small ducts (ducts of Rivinus) that open on the sublingual fold.'),
('bfa93b4f-06d9-582f-802b-2b1d2e3e7df3'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sublingual caruncle is:', '["A small papilla at the base of the frenulum of the tongue","The foramen cecum","The opening of the parotid duct","A swelling of the sublingual gland","The lingual tonsil"]'::jsonb, 'A small papilla at the base of the frenulum of the tongue', 'The sublingual caruncle (at the base of the frenulum) is the common opening of the submandibular duct and the major sublingual duct.'),
('3cc1e90a-6abe-5170-9b72-da3d0c859779'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The tongue is divided into:', '["Oral part (anterior 2/3, in the mouth) and pharyngeal part","The dorsal and ventral surfaces","The intrinsic and extrinsic parts","The tip, body, and root","The upper and lower parts"]'::jsonb, 'Oral part (anterior 2/3, in the mouth) and pharyngeal part', 'The tongue (the most muscular organ in the body relative to size) is divided by the V-shaped sulcus terminalis.'),
('85eb8109-68be-5a5c-a0c8-23280bee57e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The foramen cecum is:', '["The palatine tonsil","The lingual tonsil","The opening of the submandibular duct","A small pit at the apex of the sulcus terminalis","The opening of the parotid duct"]'::jsonb, 'A small pit at the apex of the sulcus terminalis', 'The foramen cecum (at the junction of the anterior 2/3 and posterior 1/3 of the tongue) is the remnant of the thyroglossal duct.'),
('a20dbefc-a3d4-506b-8348-b251bdfe417b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The intrinsic muscles of the tongue:', '["Elevate the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Move the tongue as a whole and its associated anatomical structures","Protrude the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Change the shape of the tongue (superior longitudinal, inferior longitudinal, transverse","Depress the tongue and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Change the shape of the tongue (superior longitudinal, inferior longitudinal, transverse', 'The intrinsic tongue muscles (CN XII) are not anchored to bone (they alter the shape of the tongue for speech and swallowing).'),
('bd9f10cd-d132-5a89-b71c-09a3be1a273f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The extrinsic muscles of the tongue:', '["Only the hyoglossus and its associated anatomical structures","Only the genioglossus and its associated anatomical structures","Only the styloglossus and its associated anatomical structures","Genioglossus, hyoglossus, styloglossus, and palatoglossus (attach the tongue to surrounding struc","Only the palatoglossus and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Genioglossus, hyoglossus, styloglossus, and palatoglossus (attach the tongue to surrounding struc', 'The genioglossus (CN XII) protrudes the tongue. The hyoglossus (CN XII) depresses the tongue. The styloglossus (CN XII) retracts the tongue. The palatoglossus (CN X) elevates the posterior tongue.'),
('5e5ae69a-0739-53b4-a5ad-7f812d8362aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The genioglossus muscle:', '["Elevates the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Shapes the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Depresses the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Protrudes the tongue (the most important muscle for tongue protrusion — CN XII","Retracts the tongue and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Protrudes the tongue (the most important muscle for tongue protrusion — CN XII', 'The genioglossus (fan-shaped, from the genial tubercle of the mandible) is the only muscle that protrudes the tongue tip.'),
('f3aa2358-6460-547a-92f0-5cc1e3800f55'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) supplies:', '["Only the extrinsic muscles and its associated anatomical structures","All muscles of the tongue including the palatoglossus and its associated anatomical structures","Only the intrinsic muscles and its associated anatomical structures","The muscles of the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","The intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue (except the palatoglossus which is supplied by"]'::jsonb, 'The intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue (except the palatoglossus which is supplied by', 'CN XII (motor to the tongue) exits the skull through the hypoglossal canal, passes between the IJV and ICA, and enters the tongue.'),
('0f3a32fd-d507-5fb5-bb51-daef815ba8f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lingual nerve (CN V3) supplies:', '["Taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue","General sensation to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue","General sensation to the posterior 1/3","Taste to the posterior 1/3","Motor to the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'General sensation to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue', 'The lingual nerve (mandibular division of CN V) carries general sensation (touch, pain, temperature) from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue.'),
('17c7f331-00c2-5d13-a626-54dcdc17f543'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The chorda tympani nerve (CN VII) supplies:', '["General sensation to the anterior 2/3 and its associated anatomical structures","Motor to the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Taste to the whole tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Taste to the posterior 1/3 and its associated anatomical structures","Taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue (and parasympathetic to the submandibular and sublingual"]'::jsonb, 'Taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue (and parasympathetic to the submandibular and sublingual', 'The chorda tympani (branch of CN VII) travels with the lingual nerve to provide taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue.'),
('9f030979-a9c8-56b2-a459-66fc1818a1cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) supplies:', '["General sensation to the anterior 2/3","Taste to the whole tongue","Taste to the anterior 2/3","Taste and general sensation to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue","Motor to the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Taste and general sensation to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue', 'CN IX (mixed nerve) supplies taste and general sensation to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue, the pharynx, and the carotid sinus and body.'),
('1de9de0f-255f-5ea5-8aea-05f71b4f6b2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The trigeminal nerve (CN V) has three divisions:', '["Upper, middle, and lower","Superior, middle, and inferior","Sensory, motor, and mixed","Anterior, middle, and posterior","Ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2), and mandibular"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2), and mandibular', 'CN V (the largest cranial nerve) provides motor innervation to the muscles of mastication and sensory innervation to the face.'),
('7d442c31-4641-5b17-b539-cda8171386c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The ophthalmic nerve (V1) supplies:', '["Taste to the anterior tongue","Sensory to the forehead, eye, nose, and the scalp","Motor to the muscles of mastication","Sensory to the jaw","Sensory to the cheek"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory to the forehead, eye, nose, and the scalp', 'V1 (ophthalmic, through the superior orbital fissure) has three branches: frontal, lacrimal, and nasociliary.'),
('2c950057-12f0-549a-957c-c4797df1a198'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The maxillary nerve (V2) supplies:', '["Sensory to the midface, maxillary sinus, upper teeth, and palate (passes through the foramen rotu","Motor to the muscles of mastication and its associated anatomical structures","Sensory to the lower teeth and its associated anatomical structures","Sensory to the forehead and its associated anatomical structures","Taste to the posterior tongue and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory to the midface, maxillary sinus, upper teeth, and palate (passes through the foramen rotu', 'V2 (maxillary, through the foramen rotundum) has the infraorbital, zygomatic, superior alveolar, and pterygopalatine branches.'),
('d60af9a7-d807-5ac5-bc30-72dd0bc85494'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The mandibular nerve (V3) supplies:', '["Sensory to the lower face, lower teeth, and anterior 2/3 of the tongue, and motor to the muscles","Motor to the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Only sensory and its associated anatomical structures","Taste to the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Only motor and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory to the lower face, lower teeth, and anterior 2/3 of the tongue, and motor to the muscles', 'V3 (mandibular, through the foramen ovale) is the only division with a motor root (the other two are purely sensory).'),
('81daf88e-36a4-51e9-a14e-b1e48dcca013'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) provides:', '["Parasympathetic only and its associated anatomical structures","Motor to the muscles of facial expression, taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue","Sensory only and its associated anatomical structures","Motor only and its associated anatomical structures","Taste only and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to the muscles of facial expression, taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue', 'CN VII (motor, sensory, and parasympathetic) exits the skull through the stylomastoid foramen.'),
('4637aeca-99e4-5f92-9ec8-2f05f8195f54'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'In Bell palsy (CN VII palsy):', '["Only the eye is affected and its associated anatomical structures","The entire face is paralyzed on one side (forehead and lower face) — the patient cannot close the","Both sides of the face are paralyzed and its associated anatomical structures","Only the lower face is paralyzed and its associated anatomical structures","Only the forehead is paralyzed and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The entire face is paralyzed on one side (forehead and lower face) — the patient cannot close the', 'Bell palsy (idiopathic CN VII palsy) causes acute unilateral facial paralysis. Forehead weakness distinguishes it from a stroke (UMN vs LMN).'),
('845c52a2-562f-5d92-98c7-58cd6f7edea8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the soft palate and pharynx are innervated by:', '["CN XII only and its associated anatomical structures","CN V only and its associated anatomical structures","The pharyngeal plexus (CN IX and CN X) — all muscles except the tensor veli palatini (V3","CN XI only and its associated anatomical structures","CN VII only and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The pharyngeal plexus (CN IX and CN X) — all muscles except the tensor veli palatini (V3', 'The pharyngeal plexus (from CN IX, CN X, and sympathetic fibers) innervates most pharyngeal and soft palate muscles.'),
('c05e595e-1dea-5972-bb43-672551d0da67'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The stylopharyngeus muscle:', '["Constricts the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing (the only muscle of the pharynx innervated by C","Opens the eustachian tube and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the palate and its associated anatomical structures","Depresses the palate and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing (the only muscle of the pharynx innervated by C', 'The stylopharyngeus (CN IX) runs from the styloid process to the pharyngeal wall (the only muscle innervated by CN IX in the pharynx).'),
('02b96eae-ed2c-5151-aa4d-e1d51e0e4f01'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pharyngeal constrictor muscles:', '["Only the superior and middle","Only the middle and inferior","Superior, middle, and inferior constrictors","All innervated by CN IX","Only the superior and inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Superior, middle, and inferior constrictors', 'The pharyngeal constrictors (circular muscles) contract sequentially to propel a bolus into the esophagus (peristalsis).'),
('70c14fbf-5ca5-51f9-9633-31d620ab8ab7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cricopharyngeus muscle:', '["The lower part of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor","Opens the esophagus and its associated anatomical structures","Closes the larynx and its associated anatomical structures","Opens the larynx and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The lower part of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor', 'The cricopharyngeus (UES) relaxes during swallowing to allow the bolus to enter the esophagus (cricopharyngeal spasm causes dysphagia).'),
('f8e906b3-9239-5817-89de-3d7479fd6b4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pyriform sinus is:', '["A recess in the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet (a common site for foreign bod","The piriform recess of the nose and its associated anatomical structures","The vallecula and its associated anatomical structures","The laryngeal ventricle and its associated anatomical structures","The sphenoid sinus and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A recess in the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet (a common site for foreign bod', 'The pyriform sinus (lateral to the larynx) is a potential site for pharyngeal cancer (pyriform sinus carcinoma).'),
('a792653e-85b3-52c4-ad6a-d6b7ad1c03fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The valleculae are:', '["Recesses between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis (on each side of the median glossoepig","The pyriform sinuses and its associated anatomical structures","Recesses in the larynx and its associated anatomical structures","The sphenoethmoidal recess and its associated anatomical structures","The lateral pharyngeal recesses and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Recesses between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis (on each side of the median glossoepig', 'The valleculae are potential sites for foreign body impaction (vallecular cysts can cause airway obstruction).'),
('2eb488dd-8c25-5c09-96a3-0d17bc32ed1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The epiglottis:', '["A hyaline cartilage that ossifies","The arytenoid cartilage","Part of the cricoid cartilage","An elastic cartilage at the base of the tongue","The thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'An elastic cartilage at the base of the tongue', 'The epiglottis (leaf-shaped) prevents aspiration during swallowing (epiglottitis is a medical emergency in children).'),
('fad56bd0-5582-5010-9c6c-6cc0369b8815'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cough reflex is mediated by:', '["The glossopharyngeal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The phrenic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The hypoglossal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The vagus nerve (CN X) — especially the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve (the affe","The facial nerve and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The vagus nerve (CN X) — especially the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve (the affe', 'The larynx (supraglottis) is the most sensitive area for triggering the cough reflex (innervated by the internal laryngeal nerve).'),
('c0dd43b3-31b9-57be-b78d-a3aab56ac93f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The gag reflex is mediated by:', '["CN X and CN XII","CN V and CN X","CN IX (afferent) and CN X","CN VII and CN IX","CN V and CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (afferent) and CN X', 'The gag reflex (touching the posterior pharyngeal wall) tests CN IX (sensory) and CN X (motor).'),
('569fac47-b0bf-56da-b24b-077ca311ee0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The carotid pulse is palpated at:', '["The midline of the neck","The posterior border of the SCM","The angle of the mandible","The suprasternal notch","The anterior border of the SCM"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior border of the SCM', 'The common carotid artery is palpated against the carotid tubercle (transverse process of C6, Chassaignac tubercle).'),
('42bd3fea-62e3-5e9d-8e2d-6cd14b4202bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cricothyroidotomy is performed at:', '["The thyroid isthmus","The trachea at the suprasternal notch","The thyrohyoid membrane","The cricotracheal membrane","The cricothyroid membrane"]'::jsonb, 'The cricothyroid membrane', 'Cricothyroidotomy (emergency surgical airway) is performed through the cricothyroid membrane (avoids the thyroid and vocal cords).'),
('f2592079-af29-5ed3-8983-e6a8a6566c32'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The thyroid cartilage is:', '["The cricoid and its associated structures","The smallest and its associated structures","The epiglottis and its associated structures","The arytenoid and its associated structures","The largest laryngeal cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'The largest laryngeal cartilage', 'The thyroid cartilage (hyaline) has two laminae that fuse at the laryngeal prominence (Adam apple) under the influence of testosterone.'),
('027bcb49-1765-5832-b04a-820bdf9c2024'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The vocal cords adduct during:', '["Respiration and its associated anatomical structures","Speech only and its associated anatomical structures","Swallowing only and its associated anatomical structures","Cough only and its associated anatomical structures","Phonation (sound production) and Valsalva maneuver"]'::jsonb, 'Phonation (sound production) and Valsalva maneuver', 'The lateral cricoarytenoid and interarytenoid muscles adduct the vocal cords; the posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor.'),
('5bf15c15-182b-53c5-a241-bb0446597908'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cricothyroid joint:', '["A synovial joint between the cricoid and the thyroid cartilage","A fibrous joint and its associated anatomical structures","A synostosis and its associated anatomical structures","A syndesmosis and its associated anatomical structures","A cartilaginous joint and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A synovial joint between the cricoid and the thyroid cartilage', 'The cricothyroid joint allows the thyroid to tilt forward or backward relative to the cricoid (changing vocal cord tension and pitch).'),
('89974c1a-ece9-5fad-a586-0f368ce5721e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cricoarytenoid joint:', '["A fibrous joint and its associated anatomical structures","A fixed joint and its associated anatomical structures","A cartilaginous joint and its associated anatomical structures","A suture and its associated anatomical structures","A synovial joint between the cricoid and the arytenoid cartilage (allows sliding and rocking move"]'::jsonb, 'A synovial joint between the cricoid and the arytenoid cartilage (allows sliding and rocking move', 'The cricoarytenoid joint (synovial) allows the arytenoid cartilage to move, adjusting the position of the vocal cords.'),
('0d428281-433e-5ee3-9404-48cc2ec65000'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The subglottis is:', '["The part of the larynx below the vocal cords","The trachea and its associated anatomical structures","Between the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures","The pyriform sinus and its associated anatomical structures","Above the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the larynx below the vocal cords', 'The subglottis (the narrowest part of the pediatric airway) is enclosed by the cricoid cartilage (a complete ring).'),
('80619dd2-e63c-512a-ad12-14f0462686be'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The supraglottis is:', '["The rima glottidis and its associated anatomical structures","The part of the larynx above the vocal cords (includes the epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds","The subglottis and its associated anatomical structures","The trachea and its associated anatomical structures","Below the vocal cords and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the larynx above the vocal cords (includes the epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds', 'The supraglottis is innervated by the internal laryngeal nerve (sensitive — triggers the cough reflex).'),
('f2e06469-6991-5dbe-9bf6-0a16327c2b54'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The laryngeal ventricle (ventricle of Morgagni):', '["The space below the glottis","The space between the true and false vocal cords","The pyriform sinus","The vallecula","The laryngeal inlet"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the true and false vocal cords', 'The laryngeal ventricle (a small recess) contains mucus-secreting glands that lubricate the vocal cords.'),
('fe40e109-bf24-53c4-afa1-e44bc73ffd90'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The saccule of the larynx (laryngeal pouch):', '["The vallecula and its associated anatomical structures","The laryngeal sinus and its associated anatomical structures","A small diverticulum at the anterior end of the laryngeal ventricle","The laryngeal ventricle itself and its associated anatomical structures","The pyriform sinus and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A small diverticulum at the anterior end of the laryngeal ventricle', 'The laryngeal saccule (sacculus laryngis) contains mucous glands. An abnormally enlarged saccule (laryngocele) causes hoarseness.'),
('796569bc-515a-56ae-85e4-578229a4d9fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the soft palate (except tensor veli palatini) are innervated by:', '["CN IX","CN XII","CN VII","CN V3","The pharyngeal plexus"]'::jsonb, 'The pharyngeal plexus', 'The tensor veli palatini (CN V3) is the only soft palate muscle not innervated by the pharyngeal plexus.'),
('daa3cf79-86a9-5326-8183-9716cd13267e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus:', '["Are innervated by CN V3 and its associated anatomical structures","Are innervated by CN VII and its associated anatomical structures","Are innervated by CN IX and its associated anatomical structures","Form the anterior and posterior tonsillar pillars respectively","Are innervated by CN XII and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Form the anterior and posterior tonsillar pillars respectively', 'The palatoglossus (anterior pillar) and palatopharyngeus (posterior pillar) are the boundaries of the tonsillar fossa.'),
('5c295873-df0b-555c-8f49-1574cd587a51'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The peritonsillar space:', '["The retropharyngeal space and its associated anatomical structures","The space between the tonsils and its associated anatomical structures","The submandibular space and its associated anatomical structures","The parapharyngeal space and its associated anatomical structures","The loose connective tissue between the palatine tonsil capsule and the superior constrictor musc"]'::jsonb, 'The loose connective tissue between the palatine tonsil capsule and the superior constrictor musc', 'Peritonsillar abscess (quinsy) causes severe throat pain, trismus, and a hot potato voice (requires incision and drainage).'),
('66285268-b09d-52e1-ba3d-26cb24fdcea2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The retropharyngeal space:', '["The parapharyngeal space and its associated anatomical structures","The space behind the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","The danger space and its associated anatomical structures","The potential space between the buccopharyngeal fascia and the prevertebral fascia (a site for re","The prevertebral space and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The potential space between the buccopharyngeal fascia and the prevertebral fascia (a site for re', 'Retropharyngeal abscess (most common in children) can cause airway obstruction (must be drained promptly).'),
('2e840e26-980f-5b75-852c-7bbb01f93d47'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The danger space (space 4 of the deep neck):', '["The retropharyngeal space and its associated anatomical structures","The prevertebral space and its associated anatomical structures","The potential space between the alar fascia and the prevertebral fascia (extends from the skull b","The parotid space and its associated anatomical structures","The submandibular space and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The potential space between the alar fascia and the prevertebral fascia (extends from the skull b', 'The danger space (the ''danger zone'' of the neck) allows infections to spread from the neck to the posterior mediastinum.'),
('870ce159-27e7-5261-8405-3210f66b1f38'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The prevertebral fascia:', '["Covers the vertebral bodies and the prevertebral muscles (from the skull base to the coccyx","Covers the carotid sheath and its associated anatomical structures","Covers the thyroid and its associated anatomical structures","Covers the trachea and its associated anatomical structures","Covers the SCM and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the vertebral bodies and the prevertebral muscles (from the skull base to the coccyx', 'The prevertebral fascia (deep cervical fascia) surrounds the deep muscles of the neck and forms the floor of the posterior triangle.'),
('777dabe6-5ab1-52c2-b43c-494ac2247fef'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pretracheal fascia:', '["Covers the trapezius and its associated anatomical structures","Covers the prevertebral muscles and its associated anatomical structures","Covers the SCM and its associated anatomical structures","Covers the thyroid gland, the trachea, and the esophagus (attached to the cricoid and the arch of","Covers the parotid gland and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the thyroid gland, the trachea, and the esophagus (attached to the cricoid and the arch of', 'The pretracheal fascia (middle layer of the deep cervical fascia) limits the spread of thyroid abscesses (downward into the mediastinum).'),
('29bbf654-ab7d-561e-9023-446aa2139027'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The investing (superficial) layer of the deep cervical fascia:', '["Covers the thyroid","Covers the prevertebral muscles","Covers the carotid sheath","Covers the trachea","Encloses the SCM and the trapezius"]'::jsonb, 'Encloses the SCM and the trapezius', 'The investing fascia (deep cervical fascia) forms the roof of the anterior and posterior triangles of the neck.'),
('ca41bef6-2076-5e5a-9c44-b65356468e03'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The buccopharyngeal fascia:', '["The thin fascia covering the pharyngeal constrictors and the buccinator muscle (continuous with t","The fascia of the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","The fascia of the mouth and its associated anatomical structures","The fascia of the esophagus and its associated anatomical structures","The fascia of the cheek and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The thin fascia covering the pharyngeal constrictors and the buccinator muscle (continuous with t', 'The buccopharyngeal fascia (posterior to the pharynx) is separated from the prevertebral fascia by the retropharyngeal space.'),
('f8e296c4-9fa2-56d4-9fbe-f397853017f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The alar fascia:', '["The same as the prevertebral fascia and its associated anatomical structures","A thin fascia between the buccopharyngeal fascia and the prevertebral fascia (the anterior bounda","The same as the pretracheal fascia and its associated anatomical structures","The same as the investing fascia and its associated anatomical structures","The same as the buccopharyngeal fascia and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fascia between the buccopharyngeal fascia and the prevertebral fascia (the anterior bounda', 'The alar fascia (a subdivision of the deep cervical fascia) is an important landmark for neck infections and surgical dissection.'),
('b1861081-a141-5b67-ad23-2a0aa7fd9def'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sympathetic trunk in the neck:', '["Has 3 cervical ganglia (superior, middle, and inferior/stellate) — lies posterior to the carotid","Has 2 ganglia and its associated anatomical structures","Has 4 ganglia and its associated anatomical structures","Has one ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","Has no ganglia and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Has 3 cervical ganglia (superior, middle, and inferior/stellate) — lies posterior to the carotid', 'The superior cervical ganglion (C1-C4) is the largest. The stellate ganglion (C8-T1) is the target for stellate ganglion block.'),
('5b4195d4-4a4f-5b1c-9d91-091fc7aeb273'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Horner syndrome involves:', '["Mydriasis, exophthalmos, and hyperhidrosis","Miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis, and enophthalmos","Ptosis and mydriasis","Miosis and hyperhidrosis","Anhidrosis and exophthalmos"]'::jsonb, 'Miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis, and enophthalmos', 'Horner syndrome (sympathetic paralysis) can be caused by a Pancoast tumor (apical lung cancer), carotid dissection, or a neck/brachial plexus injury.'),
('8ea53127-a57d-5085-b312-fac9949caacd'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The phrenic nerve is related to:', '["The middle scalene and its associated anatomical structures","The anterior scalene muscle (descends from lateral to medial, runs between the SCM and the scalen","The posterior scalene and its associated anatomical structures","The splenius capitis and its associated anatomical structures","The levator scapulae and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior scalene muscle (descends from lateral to medial, runs between the SCM and the scalen', 'The phrenic nerve (C3-C5) descends on the anterior scalene muscle, deep to the prevertebral fascia and the SCM.'),
('43ff6be4-4cdd-5cdc-a0dc-d606424cb193'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'In a surgical context, The brachial plexus is formed by:', '["The cervical plexus","The lumbar plexus","The dorsal rami of C5-T1","The ventral rami of C1-C4","The ventral rami of C5-T1"]'::jsonb, 'The ventral rami of C5-T1', 'The brachial plexus (C5-T1) has roots, trunks, divisions, cords, and terminal branches (roots lie between the scalenus anterior and medius).'),
('17f1e376-d902-5449-bade-2544f5787bb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The subclavian artery is divided into three parts by:', '["The SCM","The middle scalene","The anterior scalene muscle","The clavicle","The first rib"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior scalene muscle', 'The subclavian artery (part 1 gives off the vertebral and internal thoracic arteries; part 2 gives off the costocervical trunk; part 3 gives off the dorsal scapular artery).'),
('80252687-dd75-5bfa-b371-17115dfab2ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The vertebral artery arises from:', '["The third part of the subclavian","The second part of the subclavian","The costocervical trunk","The thyrocervical trunk","The first part of the subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'The first part of the subclavian artery', 'The vertebral artery (the first branch of the subclavian artery) enters the skull through the foramen magnum.'),
('877ae3c4-d9e6-5d71-ba82-a97c00ffe67b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The thyrocervical trunk arises from:', '["The first part of the subclavian artery (gives off the inferior thyroid, suprascapular","The vertebral artery and its associated anatomical structures","The third part of the subclavian and its associated anatomical structures","The costocervical trunk and its associated anatomical structures","The second part of the subclavian and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The first part of the subclavian artery (gives off the inferior thyroid, suprascapular', 'The thyrocervical trunk (a short, wide trunk) supplies the thyroid, the shoulder girdle, and the scapular region.'),
('b955699c-1bbe-508f-8f20-db5246411309'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The costocervical trunk arises from:', '["The internal thoracic artery and its associated anatomical structures","The first part of the subclavian and its associated anatomical structures","The thyrocervical trunk and its associated anatomical structures","The second part of the subclavian artery (gives off the superior intercostal and deep cervical ar","The third part of the subclavian and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The second part of the subclavian artery (gives off the superior intercostal and deep cervical ar', 'The costocervical trunk supplies the first two intercostal spaces and the deep neck muscles.'),
('50351294-bbab-5c07-b06a-9b471182da62'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The internal thoracic artery arises from:', '["The first part of the subclavian artery (descends behind the sternum, gives off the anterior inte","The third part and its associated anatomical structures","The thyrocervical trunk and its associated anatomical structures","The second part and its associated anatomical structures","The costocervical trunk and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The first part of the subclavian artery (descends behind the sternum, gives off the anterior inte', 'The internal thoracic artery (mammary) is used for coronary artery bypass grafting (LIMA to LAD).'),
('70a04212-0f7b-575c-9168-1fc8113e15a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The thoracic duct in the neck:', '["Drains into the right venous angle and its associated anatomical structures","Is on the right side of the neck and its associated anatomical structures","Is not present in the neck and its associated anatomical structures","Ascends from the thorax, arches lateral to the esophagus and medial to the scalenus anterior","Drains into the IJV and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Ascends from the thorax, arches lateral to the esophagus and medial to the scalenus anterior', 'The thoracic duct (the largest lymphatic vessel) arches 3-4 cm above the clavicle and drains at the junction of the left IJV and left subclavian vein.'),
('82c17c6f-91aa-5dad-81df-6e964be2fcbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'On diagnostic evaluation, The right lymphatic duct:', '["Drains only the head and its associated anatomical structures","Drains the right upper quadrant (right side of the head, neck, thorax, and right arm","Drains the entire body and its associated anatomical structures","Drains only the thorax and its associated anatomical structures","Is the same as the thoracic duct and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Drains the right upper quadrant (right side of the head, neck, thorax, and right arm', 'The right lymphatic duct (about 1 cm) drains into the right subclavian vein at the right venous angle.'),
('da48609d-378c-561c-ba71-038657ece1eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The venous angle (Pirogoff angle):', '["The angle between the SCM and the clavicle and its associated anatomical structures","The junction of the internal jugular vein and the subclavian vein (the site where the thoracic du","The angle of the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","The carotid bifurcation and its associated anatomical structures","The sternoclavicular angle and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The junction of the internal jugular vein and the subclavian vein (the site where the thoracic du', 'The venous angle (on each side) is the site where the large lymphatic trunks empty into the venous system.'),
('f2c2d381-6c46-5aea-90ad-ef96909e1a70'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The external jugular vein is formed by:', '["The maxillary and facial veins and its associated anatomical structures","The posterior auricular vein and the posterior division of the retromandibular vein (runs over th","The anterior and posterior facial veins and its associated anatomical structures","The subclavian and the transverse cervical and its associated anatomical structures","The internal jugular and the facial vein and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior auricular vein and the posterior division of the retromandibular vein (runs over th', 'The external jugular vein (EJV) crosses the SCM and drains into the subclavian vein (visible in elevated CVP).'),
('327880f0-b475-5b01-bbad-4c30e48dd159'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The internal jugular vein is formed by:', '["The subclavian vein","The confluence of the facial and retromandibular veins","The sigmoid sinus and the inferior petrosal sinus","The external jugular vein","The maxillary vein"]'::jsonb, 'The sigmoid sinus and the inferior petrosal sinus', 'The internal jugular vein (IJV) drains the dural venous sinuses (the sigmoid sinus is the direct continuation of the IJV).'),
('23e848b8-4d6b-5a22-9fe4-1bf38e721e97'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The IJV runs in the carotid sheath:', '["Lateral to the carotid artery and the vagus nerve runs between them","Medial to the carotid and its associated anatomical structures","Posterior to the carotid and its associated anatomical structures","Anterior to the carotid and its associated anatomical structures","The vagus is anterior and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to the carotid artery and the vagus nerve runs between them', 'In the carotid sheath, the IJV is lateral (superficial), the common carotid is medial (deep), and the vagus is posterior (between them).'),
('a14efe08-53bc-50a5-bc6e-b0addc143db7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The facial vein communicates with:', '["The IJV directly","The EJV","The pterygoid plexus","The subclavian vein","The cavernous sinus"]'::jsonb, 'The cavernous sinus', 'The facial vein (angular vein) connects with the cavernous sinus through the superior ophthalmic vein (infections can spread intracranially).'),
('5d5d7580-c249-5875-bf47-fd99edf1373f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pterygoid plexus of veins:', '["Drains the nasal cavity and its associated anatomical structures","Drains the orbit and its associated anatomical structures","Drains the brain and its associated anatomical structures","A venous network around the pterygoid muscles (drains into the maxillary vein","Drains the scalp and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A venous network around the pterygoid muscles (drains into the maxillary vein', 'The pterygoid plexus (in the infratemporal fossa) communicates with the cavernous sinus through the foramen ovale and foramen lacerum.'),
('ef668491-bc95-51ce-8e76-a7a7b6ee5886'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) allows:', '["Only gliding movement and its associated anatomical structures","Hinge and gliding movements (depression, elevation, protrusion, retrusion","Only hinge movement and its associated anatomical structures","Rotational movement only and its associated anatomical structures","No movement and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Hinge and gliding movements (depression, elevation, protrusion, retrusion', 'The TMJ (with an articular disc) is one of the most complex joints in the body. The disc divides the joint into upper (gliding) and lower (hinge) compartments.'),
('d44594ee-37ba-5203-99dd-8ec34a437e4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The articular disc of the TMJ:', '["Protects the joint","Stabilizes the joint","Produces synovial fluid","Divides the TMJ into upper and lower compartments","Separates the condyle from the temporal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Divides the TMJ into upper and lower compartments', 'TMJ dislocation (anterior displacement of the condyle and the disc) causes an inability to close the mouth.'),
('b1892335-fe99-5203-aecc-581ff66beb80'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The maxillary division of CN V (V2) exits the skull through:', '["The superior orbital fissure","The foramen ovale","The foramen spinosum","The foramen rotundum","The inferior orbital fissure"]'::jsonb, 'The foramen rotundum', 'V2 (maxillary nerve) passes through the foramen rotundum, the pterygopalatine fossa, and the infraorbital canal.'),
('45c2d4b1-cbac-50d9-99b2-fe50ad56585b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The infraorbital nerve (a branch of V2):', '["Supplies the forehead","Supplies the cheek","Supplies the teeth","Supplies the scalp","Exits the infraorbital foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Exits the infraorbital foramen', 'The infraorbital nerve (the terminal branch of V2) is a common site for nerve block in facial surgery.'),
('c8052a6a-e1e2-5f62-a802-e8ba80aab7af'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The mental nerve (a branch of V3):', '["Supplies the lower teeth","Exits the mental foramen","Supplies the upper lip","Supplies the cheek","Supplies the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Exits the mental foramen', 'The mental nerve (from the inferior alveolar nerve) exit the mandible through the mental foramen (at the level of the 2nd premolar).'),
('9295fc5d-b309-5ca7-ba9a-3e4a092b61a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The inferior alveolar nerve (a branch of V3):', '["Supplies the cheek","Supplies the masseter","Runs in the mandibular canal","Supplies the tongue","Supplies the upper teeth"]'::jsonb, 'Runs in the mandibular canal', 'The inferior alveolar nerve (V3) is anesthetized for dental procedures on the lower teeth (inferior alveolar nerve block).'),
('984968b2-e6be-59e4-9db1-a4f2c1fd1018'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lingual nerve (V3) supplies:', '["Taste to the anterior 2/3","General sensation to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue","Taste to the posterior 1/3","Motor to the tongue","General sensation to the posterior 1/3"]'::jsonb, 'General sensation to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue', 'The lingual nerve is at risk during wisdom tooth extraction (submandibular duct surgery or lingual nerve block).'),
('f9f90693-9fe0-58a6-a881-12d66d901770'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The chorda tympani (branch of CN VII) joins:', '["The lingual nerve (within the infratemporal fossa) to provide taste to the anterior 2/3 of the to","The hypoglossal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The vagus nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The glossopharyngeal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The facial nerve and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The lingual nerve (within the infratemporal fossa) to provide taste to the anterior 2/3 of the to', 'The chorda tympani (taste and parasympathetic) enters the pterygopalatine fossa and joins the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa.'),
('c0914a43-b4e6-5a21-9d85-6f78071b6b03'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The submandibular ganglion is:', '["A motor ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","The pterygopalatine ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A parasympathetic ganglion suspended from the lingual nerve (supplies the submandibular and subli","A sympathetic ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A sensory ganglion and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A parasympathetic ganglion suspended from the lingual nerve (supplies the submandibular and subli', 'The submandibular ganglion (parasympathetic, CN VII via the chorda tympani) provides secretomotor innervation to the submandibular and sublingual glands.'),
('e3f55758-6845-5acd-b0c0-71f4570e0c52'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pterygopalatine ganglion (sphenopalatine ganglion):', '["The submandibular ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A sensory ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A parasympathetic ganglion in the pterygopalatine fossa (supplies the lacrimal gland and the nasa","A sympathetic ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A motor ganglion and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A parasympathetic ganglion in the pterygopalatine fossa (supplies the lacrimal gland and the nasa', 'The pterygopalatine ganglion (CN VII via the greater petrosal nerve) provides secretomotor innervation to the lacrimal gland and the nasal and palatine mucosa.'),
('d85cabde-088b-5cbf-be24-de856fc91273'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The otic ganglion is:', '["A motor ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A sensory ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","The pterygopalatine ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A sympathetic ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A parasympathetic ganglion in the infratemporal fossa"]'::jsonb, 'A parasympathetic ganglion in the infratemporal fossa', 'The otic ganglion (parasympathetic, CN IX via the tympanic nerve/lesser petrosal nerve) provides secretomotor innervation to the parotid gland.'),
('77ace3b0-a669-51df-806d-5472f1c79ed1'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The ciliary ganglion is:', '["A motor ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","The pterygopalatine ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A sympathetic ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A sensory ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","A parasympathetic ganglion in the orbit (supplies the sphincter pupillae and the ciliary muscle —"]'::jsonb, 'A parasympathetic ganglion in the orbit (supplies the sphincter pupillae and the ciliary muscle —', 'The ciliary ganglion (parasympathetic, CN III) mediates the light reflex (pupillary constriction) and accommodation.'),
('8673067d-bea1-53af-a6ef-eafe09fff565'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The nasolacrimal duct:', '["Drains tears from the lacrimal sac into the inferior meatus of the nose (opens under the inferior","Drains tears onto the cheek and its associated anatomical structures","Drains tears into the superior meatus and its associated anatomical structures","Drains tears into the mouth and its associated anatomical structures","Drains tears into the throat and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Drains tears from the lacrimal sac into the inferior meatus of the nose (opens under the inferior', 'The nasolacrimal duct (1.5 cm) runs from the lacrimal sac (at the medial canthus) to the inferior meatus of the nose.'),
('48045c15-a923-56e2-94cd-ca5431188340'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lacrimal gland is located:', '["In the superolateral part of the orbit","In the maxillary sinus","In the nasal cavity","In the ethmoid sinus","In the inferomedial orbit"]'::jsonb, 'In the superolateral part of the orbit', 'The lacrimal gland (exocrine, serous) produces tears that drain through the lacrimal puncta, canaliculi, lacrimal sac, and nasolacrimal duct.'),
('e9fe7229-05fe-5420-977c-ab293eb687dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The innervation of the lacrimal gland:', '["From CN V and its associated anatomical structures","Parasympathetic from CN VII (via the pterygopalatine ganglion and the zygomatic nerve","Parasympathetic from CN III and its associated anatomical structures","Sympathetic from the stellate ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","From CN IX and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic from CN VII (via the pterygopalatine ganglion and the zygomatic nerve', 'The lacrimal gland (parasympathetic stimulation causes lacrimation; sympathetic stimulation causes vasoconstriction).'),
('6c308e43-4547-5442-9dd0-f5da0916d34d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The paranasal sinuses include:', '["Only the maxillary and its associated anatomical structures","Only the ethmoid and sphenoid and its associated anatomical structures","Only the frontal and maxillary and its associated anatomical structures","Only the frontal and its associated anatomical structures","Frontal, ethmoid (anterior, middle, posterior), sphenoid, and maxillary sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal, ethmoid (anterior, middle, posterior), sphenoid, and maxillary sinuses', 'The paranasal sinuses (air-filled cavities lined by respiratory epithelium) lighten the skull, warm/humidify air, and enhance voice resonance.'),
('8ea8caff-ba43-515f-81e5-15000bd0d166'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The maxillary sinus (antrum of Highmore):', '["The ethmoid sinus","The smallest sinus","The sphenoid sinus","The frontal sinus","The largest paranasal sinus"]'::jsonb, 'The largest paranasal sinus', 'The maxillary sinus (pyramidal) is related to the maxillary teeth (roots of the 1st and 2nd molars may protrude into the sinus).'),
('1e60d8ef-f99c-507f-857a-54944fb516d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The ostium of the maxillary sinus is:', '["In the inferior meatus and its associated anatomical structures","In the superior meatus and its associated anatomical structures","Located low on the medial wall and its associated anatomical structures","In the sphenoethmoidal recess and its associated anatomical structures","Located high on the medial wall (drains into the hiatus semilunaris of the middle meatus — ineffi"]'::jsonb, 'Located high on the medial wall (drains into the hiatus semilunaris of the middle meatus — ineffi', 'The high position of the maxillary sinus ostium (poor dependent drainage) contributes to the frequent occurrence of sinusitis.'),
('2594bed6-8f4c-56a2-982b-9980d17e288f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The hiatus semilunaris is:', '["The opening of the sphenoid sinus and its associated anatomical structures","The opening of the nasolacrimal duct and its associated anatomical structures","The opening of the eustachian tube and its associated anatomical structures","The opening of the posterior ethmoid cells and its associated anatomical structures","A curved groove in the middle meatus (the opening for the frontal sinus, maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'A curved groove in the middle meatus (the opening for the frontal sinus, maxillary sinus', 'The hiatus semilunaris (the final common drainage pathway of the anterior sinuses) is a key landmark in endoscopic sinus surgery.'),
('11600e57-68d2-5021-ae7f-7fcb21272932'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The frontal sinus drains into:', '["The middle meatus","The sphenoethmoidal recess","The superior meatus","The nasopharynx","The inferior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'The middle meatus', 'The frontal sinus (absent at birth, develops by age 7-8) drains through the frontonasal duct into the hiatus semilunaris.'),
('26f16fcc-acd9-51e5-8d40-7df2be77518d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The ethmoid sinuses (ethmoid air cells):', '["Two sinuses and its associated anatomical structures","Only anterior cells and its associated anatomical structures","Four sinuses and its associated anatomical structures","A single large sinus and its associated anatomical structures","Multiple thin-walled air cells between the orbit and the nasal cavity (divided into anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple thin-walled air cells between the orbit and the nasal cavity (divided into anterior', 'The ethmoid sinus (labyrinth) consists of 3-18 air cells. The anterior and middle cells drain into the middle meatus; the posterior cells drain into the superior meatus.'),
('31377b9d-3feb-5a73-84cb-2378a62dae29'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sphenoid sinus drains into:', '["The sphenoethmoidal recess","The nasopharynx","The inferior meatus","The middle meatus","The superior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'The sphenoethmoidal recess', 'The sphenoid sinus (above the sella turcica) is closely related to the pituitary gland, optic chiasm, and internal carotid artery.'),
('6c7989f0-e390-569f-81a6-7eae7abec772'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The conchae (turbinates) of the nose are:', '["The openings of the sinuses and its associated anatomical structures","The nasal mucosa and its associated anatomical structures","The nasal bones and its associated anatomical structures","Bony projections from the lateral nasal wall (superior, middle, and inferior conchae — the inferi","The septal cartilage and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Bony projections from the lateral nasal wall (superior, middle, and inferior conchae — the inferi', 'The middle and superior conchae are part of the ethmoid bone. The inferior concha is a separate bone.'),
('44c80b11-c790-5a46-9ed4-199cfe8a274c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The meatuses of the nose are:', '["The nasal passages and its associated anatomical structures","The spaces below each concha (inferior, middle, and superior meatuses — the sphenoethmoidal reces","The spaces between the turbinates and its associated anatomical structures","The openings of the sinuses and its associated anatomical structures","The choanae and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The spaces below each concha (inferior, middle, and superior meatuses — the sphenoethmoidal reces', 'The inferior meatus receives the nasolacrimal duct. The middle meatus receives the anterior sinuses. The superior meatus receives the posterior ethmoid cells.'),
('ad00db5f-e9e3-5cf1-b5c6-7483ef3e0036'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The nasal septum is formed by:', '["The maxilla and the palatine bones and its associated anatomical structures","Only the septal cartilage and its associated anatomical structures","Only the ethmoid bone and its associated anatomical structures","Only the vomer and its associated anatomical structures","The perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone, the vomer bone, and the septal cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'The perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone, the vomer bone, and the septal cartilage', 'The nasal septum (divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves) is often deviated (symptomatic septal deviation causes nasal obstruction).'),
('d8ea5a85-7900-5f19-b845-7d251837ca0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The roof of the nasal cavity is formed by:', '["The inferior concha and its associated anatomical structures","The vomer and its associated anatomical structures","The nasal bones, the frontal bone, the cribriform plate of the ethmoid, and the body of the sphenoid","The palatine bone and its associated anatomical structures","The maxilla and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The nasal bones, the frontal bone, the cribriform plate of the ethmoid, and the body of the sphenoid', 'The cribriform plate (ethmoid bone) transmits the olfactory nerves (CN I) — fractures can cause CSF rhinorrhea and anosmia.'),
('8a4ecab1-0ce3-5963-bea8-1f013671cb57'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The floor of the nasal cavity is formed by:', '["The palatine bone only and its associated anatomical structures","The palatine process of the maxilla and the horizontal plate of the palatine bone","The maxilla only and its associated anatomical structures","The vomer and its associated anatomical structures","The soft palate and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The palatine process of the maxilla and the horizontal plate of the palatine bone', 'The hard palate (the floor of the nose and the roof of the mouth) separates the nasal cavity from the oral cavity.'),
('33fe0cfa-fe6e-5261-aed4-88d28d2870fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The choanae (posterior nasal apertures):', '["The openings of the lacrimal ducts and its associated anatomical structures","The openings of the sinuses and its associated anatomical structures","The openings from the nasal cavity into the nasopharynx (separated by the vomer and the palatine","The openings of the eustachian tubes and its associated anatomical structures","The openings from the external nares and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The openings from the nasal cavity into the nasopharynx (separated by the vomer and the palatine', 'The choanae (paired) are the junction between the nasal cavity and the nasopharynx. Unilateral choanal atresia is a congenital obstruction.'),
('b5b2229a-edbd-575e-b2a6-8287f03ac9ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The olfactory nerve (CN I) fibers pass through:', '["The foramen rotundum","The superior orbital fissure","The foramen magnum","The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone","The optic canal"]'::jsonb, 'The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone', 'The olfactory nerve (CN I, special sensory — smell) is the only cranial nerve that can regenerate (olfactory neuroepithelial turnover).'),
('7e217840-ae24-5e97-a968-006ffe6b2651'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The optic nerve (CN II) passes through:', '["The foramen rotundum","The superior orbital fissure","The optic canal","The foramen ovale","The foramen magnum"]'::jsonb, 'The optic canal', 'CN II (special sensory — vision) is actually a tract of the brain (covered by meninges, surrounded by CSF). Increased ICP causes papilledema.'),
('8905b0a3-7e34-544f-8a3c-4c31accaeb03'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The optic chiasm is located:', '["In the occipital lobe and its associated anatomical structures","In the orbit and its associated anatomical structures","In the temporal lobe and its associated anatomical structures","At the foramen magnum and its associated anatomical structures","Above the pituitary fossa (the sella turcica) in the suprasellar cistern"]'::jsonb, 'Above the pituitary fossa (the sella turcica) in the suprasellar cistern', 'The optic chiasm (where the nasal retinal fibers decussate) is related to the pituitary gland (pituitary tumors cause bitemporal hemianopia).'),
('610f5af3-a3e6-5fe7-b2ea-6b3e99bf471b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The oculomotor nerve (CN III) passes through:', '["The superior orbital fissure (supplies all extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus and the","The foramen ovale and its associated anatomical structures","The foramen rotundum and its associated anatomical structures","The optic canal and its associated anatomical structures","The cavernous sinus and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The superior orbital fissure (supplies all extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus and the', 'CN III (motor and parasympathetic) is located in the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus. CN III palsy causes ptosis, mydriasis, and down-and-out eye position.'),
('79711a78-ee28-5eab-acd0-e075f229465e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The trochlear nerve (CN IV) passes through:', '["The foramen rotundum","The superior orbital fissure","The optic canal","The cavernous sinus","The foramen ovale"]'::jsonb, 'The superior orbital fissure', 'CN IV (the thinnest cranial nerve) supplies the superior oblique muscle (intorts the eye). CN IV palsy causes extorsion and vertical diplopia.'),
('01da2c8a-b81e-5790-99a6-0360982a44d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) passes through:', '["The optic canal","The foramen rotundum","The foramen ovale","The cavernous sinus","The superior orbital fissure"]'::jsonb, 'The superior orbital fissure', 'CN VI (motor) runs through the cavernous sinus (adjacent to the ICA). CN VI palsy (the most common isolated cranial nerve palsy) causes inability to abduct the eye.'),
('530c33dd-d78e-5240-96a7-11ac037b05e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cavernous sinus contains:', '["Only the ICA and its associated anatomical structures","CN III, CN IV, CN V1, CN V2, CN VI, and the internal carotid artery","Only the cranial nerves and its associated anatomical structures","Only the venous blood and its associated anatomical structures","Only CN VI and the ICA and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'CN III, CN IV, CN V1, CN V2, CN VI, and the internal carotid artery', 'The cavernous sinus (a paired dural venous sinus lateral to the sella turcica) is the site of cavernous sinus thrombosis (a life-threatening emergency).'),
('026412e3-eb46-5610-bd7b-656a562cfe89'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The orbit contains:', '["Only the extraocular muscles and its associated anatomical structures","Only the eyeball and its associated anatomical structures","The eyeball, extraocular muscles, cranial nerves (II, III, IV, V1, VI), the ophthalmic artery and","Only the optic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Only the lacrimal gland and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The eyeball, extraocular muscles, cranial nerves (II, III, IV, V1, VI), the ophthalmic artery and', 'The orbit (a pyramidal cavity, 30 mL volume) has a roof (frontal bone), lateral wall (zygomatic), floor (maxilla), and medial wall (ethmoid).'),
('4e92e501-faeb-553b-ac6f-36c7fa3a4baf'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The extraocular muscles include:', '["Superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, lateral rectus, superior oblique","Two obliques only and its associated anatomical structures","The levator palpebrae superioris and its associated anatomical structures","Four recti only and its associated anatomical structures","Six recti and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, lateral rectus, superior oblique', 'The six extraocular muscles (innervated by CN III, IV, VI) move the eye. The levator palpebrae superioris (CN III) elevates the eyelid.'),
('828bdb31-0d18-55c2-a730-3698fb4747ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lateral rectus muscle:', '["Elevates the eye","Depresses the eye","Abducts the eye","Adducts the eye","Rotates the eye"]'::jsonb, 'Abducts the eye', 'CN VI palsy (lateral rectus paralysis) causes esotropia (convergent squint) and inability to abduct the eye.'),
('60f213a0-d6f3-56df-b50d-33d8faa8b4c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The superior rectus muscle:', '["Extorts the eye and its associated structures","Adducts the eye and its associated structures","Depresses the eye and its associated structures","Abducts the eye and its associated structures","Elevates, adducts, and intorts the eye"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates, adducts, and intorts the eye', 'The superior rectus (CN III) elevates the eye in the abducted position.'),
('1173f8ae-3732-55a1-9495-85439ea693d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The inferior rectus muscle:', '["Elevates the eye and its associated structures","Adducts the eye and its associated structures","Depresses, adducts, and extorts the eye","Intorts the eye and its associated structures","Abducts the eye and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses, adducts, and extorts the eye', 'The inferior rectus (CN III) depresses the eye in the abducted position.'),
('5d86c581-438f-5db0-a4ad-b86eebb54211'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The medial rectus muscle:', '["Rotates the eye","Depresses the eye","Elevates the eye","Adducts the eye","Abducts the eye"]'::jsonb, 'Adducts the eye', 'The medial rectus (the strongest of the recti) is innervated by CN III (adduction is the primary action).'),
('5296e019-6939-562c-92b6-72128e422393'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The superior oblique muscle:', '["Abducts the eye and its associated structures","Intorts, depresses, and abducts the eye","Adducts the eye and its associated structures","Extorts the eye and its associated structures","Elevates the eye and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'Intorts, depresses, and abducts the eye', 'The superior oblique (CN IV) passes through the trochlea (a pulley) before inserting on the eye. CN IV palsy causes vertical diplopia worsened by looking down and in.'),
('f564eed6-5558-5d27-8bd3-8dafcda0dd1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The inferior oblique muscle:', '["Adducts the eye and its associated structures","Depresses the eye and its associated structures","Rotates the eye and its associated structures","Intorts the eye and its associated structures","Extorts, elevates, and abducts the eye"]'::jsonb, 'Extorts, elevates, and abducts the eye', 'The inferior oblique (CN III) elevates the eye in the adducted position (the only extraocular muscle that does not arise from the apex of the orbit).'),
('0251d6da-1e8c-50f6-89c7-82bca4e97c34'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'With respect to management, The levator palpebrae superioris:', '["Closes the eye","Depresses the eyelid","Elevates the eyebrow","Opens the eye","Elevates the upper eyelid"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the upper eyelid', 'The levator palpebrae superioris (CN III) is the principal elevator of the upper eyelid (ptosis in CN III palsy).'),
('15061435-8ae5-5647-93af-240c794ff85c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The tarsal plate (superior and inferior):', '["The eyelid skin and its associated anatomical structures","The conjunctiva and its associated anatomical structures","The tarsal bones and its associated anatomical structures","Dense fibrous tissue that gives form to the eyelids","The orbital septum and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Dense fibrous tissue that gives form to the eyelids', 'The tarsal plates (1 cm) are thickened fibrous sheets in the eyelids (the Meibomian glands secrete the lipid layer of the tear film).'),
('0dba0e69-cc4e-5db3-aeb3-9bce3b91aced'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The Meibomian glands:', '["Mucus glands and its associated anatomical structures","Modified sebaceous glands in the tarsal plates (secrete the lipid layer of the tear film that pre","Lacrimal glands and its associated anatomical structures","Sweat glands and its associated anatomical structures","Salivary glands and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Modified sebaceous glands in the tarsal plates (secrete the lipid layer of the tear film that pre', 'Meibomian gland dysfunction (chalazion or blepharitis) is a common eyelid condition (meibomianitis).'),
('3de9228a-eebe-58e2-a77b-164b1fd5e239'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The palpebral conjunctiva:', '["The sclera and its associated anatomical structures","The mucous membrane that lines the inner surface of the eyelids (continuous with the bulbar conju","The cornea and its associated anatomical structures","The eyelid skin and its associated anatomical structures","The tarsal plate and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The mucous membrane that lines the inner surface of the eyelids (continuous with the bulbar conju', 'The conjunctiva (a thin, transparent mucous membrane) covers the anterior sclera (bulbar) and the inner eyelids (palpebral).'),
('5bc8ddf9-d8a4-524f-b0fe-661df1f31caa'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cornea is:', '["The iris and its associated anatomical structures","The lens and its associated anatomical structures","The white part of the eye and its associated anatomical structures","The retina and its associated anatomical structures","The transparent anterior 1/6 of the outer layer of the eye (the main refractive surface"]'::jsonb, 'The transparent anterior 1/6 of the outer layer of the eye (the main refractive surface', 'The cornea (avascular, receives oxygen from the air and the aqueous humor) is innervated by CN V1 (corneal reflex).'),
('ae3f49c3-e1cc-5496-a75c-29e61e6b13cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The corneal reflex is mediated by:', '["CN II and CN VII and its associated anatomical structures","CN II and CN III and its associated anatomical structures","CN V (afferent — ophthalmic nerve) and CN VII","CN III and CN VII and its associated anatomical structures","CN V and CN III and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (afferent — ophthalmic nerve) and CN VII', 'The corneal reflex (touching the cornea causes bilateral eye closure) tests CN V (afferent) and CN VII (efferent).'),
('815945b4-e948-5e13-abf5-a21eb35c0e29'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sclera:', '["The tough, white, opaque outer layer (the posterior 5/6 of the eye — provides structural support","The transparent cornea and its associated anatomical structures","The retina and its associated anatomical structures","The choroid and its associated anatomical structures","The uvea and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The tough, white, opaque outer layer (the posterior 5/6 of the eye — provides structural support', 'The sclera (the tendon of the eye, continuous with the corneal stroma) is thinnest at the insertion of the recti.'),
('cf970d26-cad1-563a-bca2-39496c0aa387'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, The uveal tract consists of:', '["The vitreous and the aqueous","The choroid, the ciliary body, and the iris","The conjunctiva and the cornea","The retina and the lens","The sclera and the cornea"]'::jsonb, 'The choroid, the ciliary body, and the iris', 'The uvea (the vascular layer of the eye) provides blood supply to the eye and regulates the shape of the lens and the pupil.'),
('ec0dce7b-a305-53a3-8b2b-ab8cd484d247'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The iris is:', '["The colored part of the eye (a contractile diaphragm between the cornea and the lens — divides th","The pupil and its associated anatomical structures","The ciliary body and its associated anatomical structures","The lens and its associated anatomical structures","The choroid and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The colored part of the eye (a contractile diaphragm between the cornea and the lens — divides th', 'The iris (smooth muscle — dilator and sphincter pupillae) controls the size of the pupil (light reflex and accommodation).'),
('7da67704-55e6-5bda-983b-e5b507f436de'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sphincter pupillae muscle:', '["Dilates the pupil","Accommodates the eye","Controls the iris","Constricts the pupil","Changes the shape of the lens"]'::jsonb, 'Constricts the pupil', 'The sphincter pupillae (circular smooth muscle) is innervated by the parasympathetic fibers of CN III.'),
('f40aad3d-069b-5949-a5d3-d12604dbdd1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The dilator pupillae muscle:', '["Changes the shape of the lens","Dilates the pupil","Controls the iris","Constricts the pupil","Accommodates the eye"]'::jsonb, 'Dilates the pupil', 'The dilator pupillae (radial smooth muscle) is innervated by the sympathetic nervous system (mydriasis).'),
('f9603c9e-9f20-544a-ba1f-8731f13a7579'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The ciliary body:', '["The iris","The retina","The choroid","The lens","A ring-shaped structure"]'::jsonb, 'A ring-shaped structure', 'The ciliary body (pars plicata and pars plana) secretes aqueous humor into the posterior chamber (accommodation via the ciliary muscle).'),
('b46d6aff-50e7-598f-99e6-4bd4a3db9506'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lens:', '["The retina and its associated anatomical structures","The vitreous and its associated anatomical structures","The iris and its associated anatomical structures","The cornea and its associated anatomical structures","A transparent biconvex structure (focuses light on the retina — accommodation"]'::jsonb, 'A transparent biconvex structure (focuses light on the retina — accommodation', 'The lens (crystalline, avascular) is suspended by the zonular fibers (suspensory ligament). Cataract is the opacification of the lens.'),
('0cf63f55-b40a-5d6a-bf44-ee33def73d29'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The aqueous humor flows from:', '["The vitreous to the anterior chamber and its associated anatomical structures","The anterior chamber to the posterior chamber and its associated anatomical structures","The lens to the iris and its associated anatomical structures","The posterior chamber (secreted by the ciliary body), through the pupil, into the anterior chamber","The blood to the aqueous and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior chamber (secreted by the ciliary body), through the pupil, into the anterior chamber', 'The aqueous humor (maintains intraocular pressure, 10-20 mmHg) provides nutrients to the avascular cornea and lens (glaucoma blocks its drainage).'),
('c189aab7-1473-5c9a-9360-d95169ff425c'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The canal of Schlemm:', '["Drains the aqueous from the posterior chamber","Drains the vitreous","Drains tears","Drains blood from the eye","A circular channel at the iridocorneal angle"]'::jsonb, 'A circular channel at the iridocorneal angle', 'The canal of Schlemm (sinus venosus sclerae) is the main outflow pathway for the aqueous humor (obstruction causes open-angle glaucoma).'),
('ec42ad5d-5303-594e-a6ff-23f5251f7fe8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The vitreous humor:', '["The choroid and its associated anatomical structures","Aqueous humor and its associated anatomical structures","The retina and its associated anatomical structures","A transparent, gelatinous substance that fills the posterior chamber (the space between the lens","The lens and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A transparent, gelatinous substance that fills the posterior chamber (the space between the lens', 'The vitreous humor (99% water, 1% collagen and hyaluronic acid) maintains the shape of the eye and supports the retina.'),
('29cecf68-b6b1-5c74-aae3-f274d5ebbf00'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The retina:', '["The sclera","The innermost layer of the eye","The choroid","The uvea","The cornea"]'::jsonb, 'The innermost layer of the eye', 'The retina (the neural layer of the eye) has the macula (central vision, mostly cones) and the peripheral retina (rods for night vision).'),
('aef1b806-d76f-5986-a6f7-a40e7aec9c06'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The macula lutea:', '["The blind spot and its associated anatomical structures","The area of the retina for central vision (contains the fovea centralis — the highest concentrati","The optic disc and its associated anatomical structures","The peripheral retina and its associated anatomical structures","The ora serrata and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The area of the retina for central vision (contains the fovea centralis — the highest concentrati', 'The macula (yellow spot, 5 mm) is responsible for high-acuity central vision (macular degeneration causes central vision loss).'),
('a02cf668-df1e-5b17-9a36-5205313e84a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The fovea centralis:', '["The blind spot and its associated structures","A depression in the center of the macula","The ora serrata and its associated structures","The peripheral retina and its associated structures","The optic disc and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'A depression in the center of the macula', 'The fovea (0.3 mm) has the highest visual acuity (20/20). The fovea is avascular (receives oxygen from the choroid).'),
('1fb4fbea-9d7c-5b8b-b7b3-994b8f914289'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The optic disc:', '["The ora serrata","The macula","The fovea","The area where the optic nerve leaves the eye","The area of sharpest vision"]'::jsonb, 'The area where the optic nerve leaves the eye', 'The optic disc (optic nerve head) is visible on fundoscopy. Papilledema (swelling of the optic disc) indicates increased intracranial pressure.'),
('72897cc2-9f08-5ed4-823a-8c9ea4179793'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The rods of the retina:', '["For high acuity and its associated anatomical structures","For color vision and its associated anatomical structures","For day vision and its associated anatomical structures","For central vision and its associated anatomical structures","Photoreceptors for night vision (scotopic — more sensitive to light, located in the peripheral re"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptors for night vision (scotopic — more sensitive to light, located in the peripheral re', 'Rods (120 million) contain rhodopsin (visual purple) and are more numerous than cones (6 million). Night blindness (nyctalopia) results from rod dysfunction.'),
('6ba43b66-9a0f-5b5a-a5d5-609c4bb09ad4'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cones of the retina:', '["For dim light","For motion detection","Photoreceptors for color vision","For night vision","For peripheral vision"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptors for color vision', 'Cones (6 million) contain three photopsins (red, green, blue cones) for color vision. Cone dysfunction causes color blindness.'),
('64a870f7-140c-51e4-ac5e-b41709280979'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The visual pathway:', '["Retina → lateral geniculate → optic tract and its associated anatomical structures","Retina → optic nerve → optic chiasm → optic tract → lateral geniculate body → optic radiation → p","Retina → optic chiasm → optic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","Retina → visual cortex directly and its associated anatomical structures","Retina → thalamus → cortex and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Retina → optic nerve → optic chiasm → optic tract → lateral geniculate body → optic radiation → p', 'The visual pathway (from the eye to the occipital lobe) has a specific topographic organization (macular fibers are in the temporal side of the optic nerve).'),
('8aee9613-2556-54b0-8d79-e85bffa0658e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pupillary light reflex:', '["Afferent: CN VI, efferent: CN III and its associated anatomical structures","Afferent: CN III, efferent: CN II and its associated anatomical structures","Afferent: CN V, efferent: CN VII and its associated anatomical structures","Afferent: CN II, efferent: CN IV and its associated anatomical structures","Direct and consensual pupillary constriction in response to light (afferent: CN II"]'::jsonb, 'Direct and consensual pupillary constriction in response to light (afferent: CN II', 'The light reflex tests the integrity of CN II and CN III (the afferent limb is CN II; the efferent limb is CN III to the sphincter pupillae).'),
('adf929f9-677d-5728-bd68-416917e29571'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The efferent limb of the light reflex:', '["CN III (parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to the ciliary ganglion to the s","CN IV and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","CN II and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","CN V and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","CN VI and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'CN III (parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to the ciliary ganglion to the s', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus (midbrain) sends preganglionic parasympathetic fibers in CN III to the ciliary ganglion.'),
('f928b170-30e6-5512-84be-38a64c4c9f23'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The accommodation reflex:', '["Afferent: CN V, efferent: CN VII","Convergence, pupillary constriction, and lens thickening","Afferent: CN VI, efferent: CN III","Afferent: CN II, efferent: CN IV","Afferent: CN III, efferent: CN II"]'::jsonb, 'Convergence, pupillary constriction, and lens thickening', 'The accommodation reflex allows the eye to focus on near objects (ciliary muscle contraction, lens rounding, pupillary constriction, convergence).'),
('8b7e6b5f-e97c-5f02-81c8-3105aeae8465'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The trigeminal nerve (CN V) exits the pons:', '["As two sensory roots and its associated anatomical structures","As three separate roots and its associated anatomical structures","As two motor roots and its associated anatomical structures","As a large sensory root and a small motor root","As a single root and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'As a large sensory root and a small motor root', 'CN V (the largest cranial nerve) has three sensory nuclei (mesencephalic, principal, spinal) and one motor nucleus.'),
('abc52eb5-07f3-59a5-bede-1456429757a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The spinal trigeminal nucleus receives:', '["Taste fibers and its associated anatomical structures","Motor fibers and its associated anatomical structures","Proprioceptive fibers and its associated anatomical structures","Touch fibers and its associated anatomical structures","Pain and temperature fibers from the face"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature fibers from the face', 'The spinal trigeminal nucleus (extends from the pons to the upper cervical cord) processes facial pain and temperature.'),
('280dd23f-ab29-57ca-8a94-e6b7b5346a8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The trigeminal ganglion (semilunar/Gasserian ganglion):', '["The otic ganglion and its associated anatomical structures","The motor nucleus of CN V and its associated anatomical structures","A large sensory ganglion in Meckel cave (on the petrous temporal bone, contains the cell bodies o","The mesencephalic nucleus and its associated anatomical structures","The spinal nucleus and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A large sensory ganglion in Meckel cave (on the petrous temporal bone, contains the cell bodies o', 'The trigeminal ganglion (Gasserian ganglion) is located in the middle cranial fossa (trigeminal neuralgia is treated with ganglion ablation).'),
('532d15ed-96a7-5e1b-b30a-b4529adfe2b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Trigeminal neuralgia (tic douloureux):', '["Severe, paroxysmal, lancinating pain in the trigeminal nerve distribution (usually V2 or V3","A motor disorder of CN V and its associated anatomical structures","Facial muscle paralysis and its associated anatomical structures","A headache disorder and its associated anatomical structures","A loss of facial sensation and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Severe, paroxysmal, lancinating pain in the trigeminal nerve distribution (usually V2 or V3', 'Trigeminal neuralgia (the most painful condition in medicine) is often caused by vascular compression of the trigeminal nerve root.'),
('4c2fd233-c71f-58a1-8e44-341e7049bf4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) has five terminal branches:', '["Frontal, maxillary, mandibular, and cervical","Temporal, frontal, nasal, oral, and mental","Upper, middle, and lower divisions","Temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical","Anterior, posterior, superior, and inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical', 'The five terminal branches of CN VII (TZBMaC — To Zanzibar By Motor Car) supply the muscles of facial expression.'),
('7cedf303-5fc5-50f6-96f4-f9e9f8ab23e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The marginal mandibular branch of CN VII:', '["Supplies the orbicularis oris","Supplies the depressor anguli oris and the mentalis","Supplies the frontalis","Supplies the orbicularis oculi","Supplies the platysma"]'::jsonb, 'Supplies the depressor anguli oris and the mentalis', 'The marginal mandibular nerve (CN VII) runs along the mandible (below the mandibular border) and is vulnerable in submandibular surgery.'),
('81575986-bb8c-53ff-801b-7996e263db84'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) has:', '["Only sensory and its associated anatomical structures","Only motor and its associated anatomical structures","Motor (stylopharyngeus), sensory (posterior tongue, pharynx), parasympathetic (parotid via otic g","Only parasympathetic and its associated anatomical structures","Only taste and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Motor (stylopharyngeus), sensory (posterior tongue, pharynx), parasympathetic (parotid via otic g', 'CN IX (mixed nerve) exits the skull through the jugular foramen with CN X and CN XI.'),
('c1dd5cdc-1683-5a92-a484-bddcd31e4f27'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The vagus nerve (CN X) has:', '["Only sensory and its associated anatomical structures","Only motor and its associated anatomical structures","Only motor and sensory and its associated anatomical structures","Only parasympathetic and its associated anatomical structures","Motor to the palate, pharynx, and larynx; sensory to the pharynx, larynx"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to the palate, pharynx, and larynx; sensory to the pharynx, larynx', 'CN X (the longest cranial nerve) extends from the medulla to the abdomen. Vagal injury causes hoarseness, dysphagia, and aspiration.'),
('0b6d4d23-45e8-5f85-9ab1-c1ffddd1e30a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN) is a branch of:', '["The glossopharyngeal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The vagus nerve (CN X) — loops around the aortic arch (left) or the subclavian artery","The hypoglossal nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The facial nerve and its associated anatomical structures","The accessory nerve and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The vagus nerve (CN X) — loops around the aortic arch (left) or the subclavian artery', 'The left RLN loops around the aortic arch (at the ligamentum arteriosum). The right RLN loops around the right subclavian artery.'),
('2592b176-695c-5de9-bcd5-c1f585183343'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The RLN innervates:', '["The palate and its associated anatomical structures","The pharyngeal constrictors and its associated anatomical structures","The cricothyroid and its associated anatomical structures","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid (affected in thyroid surgery — causes voc","The tongue and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid (affected in thyroid surgery — causes voc', 'Unilateral RLN injury causes hoarseness (the vocal cord is paramedian). Bilateral RLN injury causes stridor and airway obstruction.'),
('ab9deac3-f295-5359-b98b-d960d2449194'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The external laryngeal nerve (branch of the superior laryngeal nerve):', '["Supplies the supraglottic mucosa and its associated anatomical structures","Supplies all intrinsic muscles and its associated anatomical structures","Supplies the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","Supplies the subglottic mucosa and its associated anatomical structures","Supplies the cricothyroid muscle (tenses the vocal cords — injury causes voice fatigue and loss o"]'::jsonb, 'Supplies the cricothyroid muscle (tenses the vocal cords — injury causes voice fatigue and loss o', 'The external laryngeal nerve (ELN) is vulnerable during ligation of the superior thyroid artery (in thyroid surgery).'),
('70ebb041-3b38-5509-8047-6a4c717a3eb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, The accessory nerve (CN XI):', '["A parasympathetic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","A pure motor nerve (supplies the SCM and the trapezius muscle — exits the skull through the jugul","A mixed nerve and its associated anatomical structures","A special sensory nerve and its associated anatomical structures","A sensory nerve and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A pure motor nerve (supplies the SCM and the trapezius muscle — exits the skull through the jugul', 'CN XI (spinal accessory nerve) is formed by cranial (from the vagus) and spinal roots (from the upper cervical cord). The spinal root is the principal part.'),
('eccf11e1-d149-5587-a888-f76f301360d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII):', '["A special sensory nerve and its associated anatomical structures","A sensory nerve and its associated anatomical structures","A pure motor nerve (supplies the intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles — passes through the hypo","A parasympathetic nerve and its associated anatomical structures","A mixed nerve and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A pure motor nerve (supplies the intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles — passes through the hypo', 'CN XII (motor to the tongue) deviates toward the side of the lesion (the genioglossus protrudes the tongue toward the contralateral side).'),
('3654a705-8444-5fb1-bb8c-14d5e4940a1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sternocleidomastoid reflex tests:', '["CN XII and its associated anatomical structures","CN X and its associated anatomical structures","CN IX and its associated anatomical structures","CN XI (spinal accessory nerve) — the SCM rotates the head to the opposite side","CN VII and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'CN XI (spinal accessory nerve) — the SCM rotates the head to the opposite side', 'The SCM and the trapezius are tested by asking the patient to turn the head against resistance or shrug the shoulders.'),
('3a5f2c65-3381-5efe-bbef-57d2cb586bdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The jaw jerk reflex tests:', '["The motor and sensory roots of CN V (trigeminal nerve) — the masseter and temporalis contract","CN XII and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","CN IX and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","CN X and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures","CN VII and its associated anatomical structures and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'The motor and sensory roots of CN V (trigeminal nerve) — the masseter and temporalis contract', 'The jaw jerk (masseter reflex) is a monosynaptic reflex (the afferent and efferent fibers are in CN V3). An exaggerated jaw jerk indicates UMN lesion.'),
('249e3d87-540a-5aa7-925f-66823aa26c63'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the face (muscles of facial expression) are:', '["Muscles of mastication","Extraocular muscles","Tongue muscles","Deep muscles of the face","Subcutaneous muscles that move the skin"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous muscles that move the skin', 'The muscles of facial expression (CN VII) include the orbicularis oris, orbicularis oculi, frontalis, buccinator, platysma, and the perioral muscles.'),
('70c03d03-a013-5728-ba7a-705ad1450f1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The orbicularis oculi:', '["Depresses the eyelid and its associated anatomical structures","Opens the eyelid and its associated anatomical structures","Moves the eyebrow and its associated anatomical structures","Closes the eyelid (palpebral part for gentle closure, orbital part for forceful closure","Elevates the eyelid and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Closes the eyelid (palpebral part for gentle closure, orbital part for forceful closure', 'The orbicularis oculi (CN VII) is tested by asking the patient to close the eyes tightly. Inability to close the eye causes corneal exposure.'),
('5f46e58b-b921-54f7-afce-d73b6cd4092a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The orbicularis oris:', '["Closes the jaw and its associated structures","Depresses the lip and its associated structures","Opens the mouth and its associated structures","A circular muscle around the mouth","Elevates the lip and its associated structures"]'::jsonb, 'A circular muscle around the mouth', 'The orbicularis oris (the kisser muscle) is important for speech (bilabial sounds) and facial expression.'),
('f3ed5564-eccd-56c8-b96b-d22077e75fd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The buccinator muscle:', '["Retracts the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","Protrudes the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Compresses the cheek against the teeth (prevents food from pooling in the vestibule — the trumpet","Depresses the mandible and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Compresses the cheek against the teeth (prevents food from pooling in the vestibule — the trumpet', 'The buccinator (CN VII) forms the lateral wall of the oral cavity and is pierced by the parotid duct.'),
('602bb717-c275-51c0-8fb8-c972e2ec3991'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The platysma muscle:', '["Elevates the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","Depresses the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","Rotates the head and its associated anatomical structures","Extends the neck and its associated anatomical structures","A thin sheet of muscle in the superficial fascia of the neck (wrinkles the neck skin — innervated"]'::jsonb, 'A thin sheet of muscle in the superficial fascia of the neck (wrinkles the neck skin — innervated', 'The platysma (CN VII) is a vestigial muscle in humans (well-developed in animals for skin twitching). Platysmaplasty is a cosmetic neck procedure.'),
('00dafd8d-bbf5-5b52-bf08-65420343bae7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The frontalis (occipitofrontalis) muscle:', '["Elevates the eyebrow (wrinkles the forehead — the frontal belly of the epicranius","Closes the eye and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the eyelid and its associated anatomical structures","Moves the scalp and its associated anatomical structures","Depresses the eyebrow and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the eyebrow (wrinkles the forehead — the frontal belly of the epicranius', 'The frontalis (CN VII) raises the eyebrow (horizontal forehead wrinkles). Bilateral frontalis weakness causes brow ptosis.'),
('a90bdaec-5496-5802-bc9a-1f9bd458d064'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The occipitofrontalis muscle:', '["A muscle of the neck and its associated anatomical structures","A muscle of the eye and its associated anatomical structures","A muscle of the face and its associated anatomical structures","A muscle of mastication and its associated anatomical structures","A broad muscular sheet covering the scalp (frontal and occipital bellies connected by the galea a"]'::jsonb, 'A broad muscular sheet covering the scalp (frontal and occipital bellies connected by the galea a', 'The occipitofrontalis (CN VII) moves the scalp (the epicranial aponeurosis allows the scalp to glide over the skull).'),
('5d67a30d-47c5-5d5a-b818-e0fd297b69ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The galea aponeurotica (epicranial aponeurosis):', '["The temporalis fascia and its associated anatomical structures","The skin of the scalp and its associated anatomical structures","The pericranium and its associated anatomical structures","The loose areolar tissue and its associated anatomical structures","The tough fibrous layer that connects the frontal and occipital bellies of the occipitofrontalis"]'::jsonb, 'The tough fibrous layer that connects the frontal and occipital bellies of the occipitofrontalis', 'The scalp has five layers (S: skin, C: connective tissue, A: aponeurosis/galea, L: loose areolar tissue, P: pericranium).'),
('a593acab-cce4-55bb-ae8b-becd631839b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The dangerous layer of the scalp is:', '["The loose areolar layer (layer 4 — infections spread through emissary veins to the intracranial v","The skin and its associated anatomical structures","The pericranium and its associated anatomical structures","The galea and its associated anatomical structures","The connective tissue and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The loose areolar layer (layer 4 — infections spread through emissary veins to the intracranial v', 'The loose areolar layer (the dangerous layer) of the scalp allows the spread of infection and is the plane of dissection in scalp flaps.'),
('2a642e03-4ced-52ec-a377-c634427c340e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The emissary veins:', '["Drain the scalp and its associated anatomical structures","Connect the intracranial venous sinuses to the extracranial veins (a route for infection to sprea","Drain the meninges and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the muscles and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the bones and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Connect the intracranial venous sinuses to the extracranial veins (a route for infection to sprea', 'The emissary veins (valveless) pass through the skull (the mastoid emissary vein connects the sigmoid sinus to the posterior auricular vein).'),
('f526fd73-5149-598a-93e5-399ffac93ecf'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pterygopalatine fossa:', '["The orbital cavity and its associated anatomical structures","The nasal cavity and its associated anatomical structures","A small pyramidal space behind the maxilla (contains the maxillary nerve, pterygopalatine ganglion","The temporal fossa and its associated anatomical structures","The infratemporal fossa and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A small pyramidal space behind the maxilla (contains the maxillary nerve, pterygopalatine ganglion', 'The pterygopalatine fossa (the crossroads of the face) communicates with the orbit, nasal cavity, oral cavity, and the cranial cavity.'),
('6ff22852-75b4-5466-9e31-848e801c1ad7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The infratemporal fossa:', '["The submandibular space and its associated anatomical structures","The temporal fossa and its associated anatomical structures","The cavernous sinus and its associated anatomical structures","The space behind the maxilla and below the skull base (contains the mandibular nerve","The pterygopalatine fossa and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The space behind the maxilla and below the skull base (contains the mandibular nerve', 'The infratemporal fossa (the region deep to the ramus of the mandible) communicates with the pterygopalatine fossa through the pterygomaxillary fissure.'),
('7604c6d2-a244-5c65-9b8e-798875d6efa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pterygomaxillary fissure:', '["The foramen rotundum and its associated anatomical structures","The inferior orbital fissure and its associated anatomical structures","A vertical gap between the maxilla and the pterygoid process of the sphenoid (connects the infrat","The sphenopalatine foramen and its associated anatomical structures","The pterygopalatine canal and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A vertical gap between the maxilla and the pterygoid process of the sphenoid (connects the infrat', 'The pterygomaxillary fissure transmits the maxillary artery (from the infratemporal fossa to the pterygopalatine fossa).'),
('289d7c7b-af1a-5074-93cc-88ab97b97979'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sphenopalatine foramen:', '["The opening of the sphenoid sinus and its associated anatomical structures","The pterygoid canal and its associated anatomical structures","The foramen rotundum and its associated anatomical structures","The opening between the pterygopalatine fossa and the nasal cavity (transmits the sphenopalatine","The greater palatine foramen and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The opening between the pterygopalatine fossa and the nasal cavity (transmits the sphenopalatine', 'The sphenopalatine foramen (the foramen of the pterygopalatine fossa) is the site of a sphenopalatine artery ligation for posterior epistaxis.'),
('d2dbe835-761d-5b04-8e9c-26082388ed00'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The greater palatine foramen:', '["The incisive foramen and its associated anatomical structures","The lesser palatine foramen and its associated anatomical structures","The pterygoid canal and its associated anatomical structures","The opening in the posterolateral hard palate (transmits the greater palatine nerve and vessels t","The sphenopalatine foramen and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The opening in the posterolateral hard palate (transmits the greater palatine nerve and vessels t', 'The greater palatine foramen (at the junction of the maxilla and the palatine bone) is a landmark for maxillary nerve block.'),
('fee12814-3617-569c-a1a1-a3ada1fc33f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lesser palatine foramina:', '["In the incisive fossa","Posterior to the greater palatine foramen","In the pterygopalatine fossa","In the nasal cavity","In the sphenoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the greater palatine foramen', 'The lesser palatine nerves (CN V2 via the pterygopalatine ganglion) supply the soft palate.'),
('663059ec-31be-59fc-b1cf-dbca283b6f47'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The incisive foramen:', '["The sphenopalatine foramen and its associated anatomical structures","The greater palatine foramen and its associated anatomical structures","The lesser palatine foramen and its associated anatomical structures","A midline opening behind the incisor teeth (transmits the nasopalatine nerve and the descending p","The mental foramen and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A midline opening behind the incisor teeth (transmits the nasopalatine nerve and the descending p', 'The incisive foramen (incisive canal) connects the nasal cavity to the oral cavity (the nasopalatine nerve supplies the anterior hard palate).'),
('15180251-219d-5652-9119-8349e9e8110a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The mandibular canal:', '["A bony canal in the mandible (contains the inferior alveolar nerve and vessels — runs from the ma","The pterygomaxillary fissure and its associated anatomical structures","The maxillary sinus and its associated anatomical structures","The incisive canal and its associated anatomical structures","The nasolacrimal duct and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A bony canal in the mandible (contains the inferior alveolar nerve and vessels — runs from the ma', 'The mandibular foramen (on the medial surface of the ramus) is the entry of the inferior alveolar nerve (target for inferior alveolar nerve block).'),
('6ce4bb47-f793-5f4a-be64-a024354f3ed6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lingula of the mandible:', '["The condylar process and its associated anatomical structures","The mandibular notch and its associated anatomical structures","A small bony prominence at the mandibular foramen","The coronoid process and its associated anatomical structures","The mental protuberance and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A small bony prominence at the mandibular foramen', 'The lingula (a sharp spine) overhangs the mandibular foramen (a landmark for inferior alveolar nerve block).'),
('36b14cae-f6a2-5e4d-b905-4db7d31bf214'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The temporalis muscle inserts on:', '["The mental protuberance and its associated anatomical structures","The alveolar process and its associated anatomical structures","The coronoid process and the anterior ramus of the mandible","The angle of the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","The condylar process and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The coronoid process and the anterior ramus of the mandible', 'The temporalis (a fan-shaped muscle) inserts on the coronoid process of the mandible (a key landmark for the temporal approach).'),
('77aab6c8-4002-5b65-abc6-fe2c89b606ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lateral pterygoid plate:', '["The pterygoid process","The maxillary tuberosity","The sphenoid spine","A bony projection of the sphenoid bone","The pterygoid hamulus"]'::jsonb, 'A bony projection of the sphenoid bone', 'The lateral pterygoid plate (two plates: lateral and medial) separates the infratemporal fossa from the pterygopalatine fossa.'),
('e0f03c58-701d-5ffa-a556-e5f2c2d4d6ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pterygoid hamulus:', '["The pterygoid process and its associated anatomical structures","A small hook at the lower end of the medial pterygoid plate (the tensor veli palatini tendon wrap","The lateral pterygoid plate and its associated anatomical structures","The sphenoid spine and its associated anatomical structures","The maxillary tuberosity and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A small hook at the lower end of the medial pterygoid plate (the tensor veli palatini tendon wrap', 'The pterygoid hamulus (a hook-like process) is an important landmark for cleft palate surgery.'),
('4b66704f-83eb-5d32-8dd6-30e071cee7d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sphenomandibular ligament:', '["The pterygospinous ligament and its associated anatomical structures","The stylohyoid ligament and its associated anatomical structures","The temporomandibular ligament and its associated anatomical structures","A fibrous band from the spine of the sphenoid to the lingula of the mandible (a remnant of the fi","The stylomandibular ligament and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous band from the spine of the sphenoid to the lingula of the mandible (a remnant of the fi', 'The sphenomandibular ligament (the primary passive support of the mandible) is a landmark for inferior alveolar nerve block.'),
('f1148d0b-84e0-511c-b1ee-96ae4193b7c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The stylomandibular ligament:', '["A thickened band of the deep cervical fascia from the styloid process to the angle of the mandibl","The temporomandibular ligament and its associated anatomical structures","The stylohyoid ligament and its associated anatomical structures","The sphenomandibular ligament and its associated anatomical structures","The pterygospinous ligament and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A thickened band of the deep cervical fascia from the styloid process to the angle of the mandibl', 'The stylomandibular ligament (between the parotid and submandibular glands) is a surgical landmark in neck dissection.'),
('d01b6bde-e708-5126-af9d-126f9584c929'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The mylohyoid muscle:', '["Elevates the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Forms the floor of the mouth (a sling from the mylohyoid line of the mandible to the hyoid bone —","Depresses the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","Protrudes the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the hyoid and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Forms the floor of the mouth (a sling from the mylohyoid line of the mandible to the hyoid bone —', 'The mylohyoid (CN V3) is a key muscle in the floor of the mouth (forms the oral diaphragm). The submandibular duct and lingual nerve lie on its superior surface.'),
('35a18419-8575-5b36-bf8e-b02ead6ade92'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The geniohyoid muscle:', '["Runs from the genial tubercle to the hyoid bone (elevates the hyoid and the tongue — innervated b","Opens the mouth and its associated anatomical structures","Protrudes the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Depresses the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the tongue and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Runs from the genial tubercle to the hyoid bone (elevates the hyoid and the tongue — innervated b', 'The geniohyoid (C1 via CN XII) is an accessory muscle of mastication and swallowing.'),
('c23708f5-a009-524c-860a-cd91706a812f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The stylohyoid muscle:', '["Protrudes the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Runs from the styloid process to the hyoid bone (elevates and retracts the hyoid — innervated by","Depresses the mandible and its associated anatomical structures","Opens the mouth and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the pharynx and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Runs from the styloid process to the hyoid bone (elevates and retracts the hyoid — innervated by', 'The stylohyoid (CN VII) is the only suprahyoid muscle innervated by CN VII (the digastric posterior belly is also CN VII).'),
('39cc37ae-09d1-512a-b25d-1976b0edc559'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The digastric muscle:', '["Is innervated by CN VII only and its associated anatomical structures","Is innervated by CN IX and its associated anatomical structures","Is innervated by CN V3 only and its associated anatomical structures","Is innervated by CN XII and its associated anatomical structures","Has an anterior belly (CN V3) and a posterior belly (CN VII) — elevates the hyoid and depresses t"]'::jsonb, 'Has an anterior belly (CN V3) and a posterior belly (CN VII) — elevates the hyoid and depresses t', 'The digastric (two bellies, innervated by two different nerves) is a landmark for submandibular and carotid triangle dissection.'),
('4d1a3b2f-4375-5fc4-abbc-8da47e7ee4e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The hyoglossus muscle:', '["Elevates the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Depresses and retracts the tongue (from the hyoid bone to the side of the tongue — innervated by","Protrudes the tongue and its associated anatomical structures","Elevates the hyoid and its associated anatomical structures","Opens the mouth and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses and retracts the tongue (from the hyoid bone to the side of the tongue — innervated by', 'The hyoglossus (CN XII) is a landmark for the lingual artery (which runs deep to the hyoglossus).'),
('e7f4297b-941d-55bf-8bcd-454538b0b112'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The styloglossus muscle:', '["Elevates the hyoid","Depresses the tongue","Retracts the tongue","Elevates the tongue","Protrudes the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Retracts the tongue', 'The styloglossus (CN XII) retracts the tongue and elevates the lateral tongue for swallowing.'),
('1e7c1d98-ebc0-5378-91c4-7ecc60e532a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the pharynx (pharyngeal constrictors):', '["All innervated by CN IX and its associated anatomical structures","Only the middle and inferior and its associated anatomical structures","Only the superior and middle and its associated anatomical structures","Only the superior and inferior and its associated anatomical structures","Superior, middle, and inferior constrictors (contract sequentially for peristalsis — innervated b"]'::jsonb, 'Superior, middle, and inferior constrictors (contract sequentially for peristalsis — innervated b', 'The inferior constrictor (the thickest) overlaps the middle constrictor, which overlaps the superior constrictor (like stacked cups).'),
('739a71ec-766e-5489-ae06-67303f9ac4e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pharyngeal raphe:', '["The palatopharyngeal arch and its associated anatomical structures","The cricopharyngeal ligament and its associated anatomical structures","The pharyngeal plexus and its associated anatomical structures","The pharyngeal recess and its associated anatomical structures","The median fibrous raphe at the posterior midline of the pharynx (the attachment of the pharyngea"]'::jsonb, 'The median fibrous raphe at the posterior midline of the pharynx (the attachment of the pharyngea', 'The pharyngeal raphe (on the prevertebral fascia) is the posterior attachment of the pharyngeal constrictor muscles.'),
('ccba670b-71be-56c0-a83f-0be594508410'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The pharyngobasilar fascia:', '["The buccopharyngeal fascia and its associated anatomical structures","The muscular layer and its associated anatomical structures","The pretracheal fascia and its associated anatomical structures","The mucosa and its associated anatomical structures","The fibrous layer of the pharyngeal wall (superior to the superior constrictor — attaches the pha"]'::jsonb, 'The fibrous layer of the pharyngeal wall (superior to the superior constrictor — attaches the pha', 'The pharyngobasilar fascia (the internal fibrous layer) fills the gap above the superior constrictor (the sinus of Morgagni).'),
('c423ac07-3bb6-5195-aacd-6295cfb47778'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sinus of Morgagni (pharyngeal recess):', '["A weak area in the pharyngeal wall above the superior constrictor (a site of nasopharyngeal carci","The vallecula and its associated anatomical structures","The pyriform sinus and its associated anatomical structures","The pharyngeal recess and its associated anatomical structures","The sphenoethmoidal recess and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A weak area in the pharyngeal wall above the superior constrictor (a site of nasopharyngeal carci', 'The sinus of Morgagni (lateral pharyngeal recess) is the most common site of origin for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.'),
('e3c3f05c-4fd3-5457-9dc6-02c5a7184067'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The branchial arches (pharyngeal arches):', '["Eight arches and its associated anatomical structures","Six arches in the embryo (arch 1: mandibular; arch 2: hyoid; arch 3: stylopharyngeus","Five arches and its associated anatomical structures","Seven arches and its associated anatomical structures","Four arches and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Six arches in the embryo (arch 1: mandibular; arch 2: hyoid; arch 3: stylopharyngeus', 'The branchial arches (mesodermal cores with a nerve, artery, and cartilage) form the structures of the face, neck, and pharynx.'),
('2ba378ae-75eb-5b9e-81a8-ba798863eb37'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The first branchial arch (mandibular arch) forms:', '["The laryngeal cartilages and its associated anatomical structures","The facial muscles and its associated anatomical structures","The hyoid bone and its associated anatomical structures","The mandible, maxilla, incus, malleus, the muscles of mastication","The stylohyoid ligament and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The mandible, maxilla, incus, malleus, the muscles of mastication', 'The first arch (Meckel cartilage) is innervated by CN V3. First arch syndrome (Treacher Collins syndrome) causes facial deformities.'),
('17a32d0a-3c74-52b2-b5f8-98ace552e94f'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The second branchial arch (hyoid arch) forms:', '["The mandible and its associated anatomical structures","The laryngeal cartilages and its associated anatomical structures","The incus and malleus and its associated anatomical structures","The stapes, the styloid process, the stylohyoid ligament, the lesser horn of the hyoid","The tongue and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The stapes, the styloid process, the stylohyoid ligament, the lesser horn of the hyoid', 'The second arch (Reichert cartilage) is innervated by CN VII. Second arch anomalies cause branchial cleft cysts (along the anterior border of the SCM).'),
('0097b557-5f8c-5c9a-a6bd-d72aa35d190e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The third branchial arch forms:', '["The facial muscles and its associated anatomical structures","The laryngeal cartilages and its associated anatomical structures","The mandible and its associated anatomical structures","The stapes and its associated anatomical structures","The greater horn of the hyoid bone and the stylopharyngeus muscle"]'::jsonb, 'The greater horn of the hyoid bone and the stylopharyngeus muscle', 'The third arch (innervated by CN IX) contributes to the root of the tongue and the pharyngeal wall.'),
('a8fbe5c9-d5d6-5378-8a32-c2e9d3c9c45d'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The fourth branchial arch forms:', '["The thyroid cartilage, the epiglottis, and the laryngeal muscles (innervated by the superior lary","The facial muscles and its associated anatomical structures","The hyoid bone and its associated anatomical structures","The palate and its associated anatomical structures","The jaw and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The thyroid cartilage, the epiglottis, and the laryngeal muscles (innervated by the superior lary', 'The fourth arch (superior laryngeal nerve, CN X) forms the cricothyroid muscle and the pharyngeal constrictors.'),
('d478fd73-4ff9-560e-a56b-99865986baaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sixth branchial arch forms:', '["The hyoid bone and its associated anatomical structures","The cricoid, arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages (innervated by the recurrent laryng","The epiglottis and its associated anatomical structures","The stapes and its associated anatomical structures","The thyroid cartilage and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The cricoid, arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages (innervated by the recurrent laryng', 'The sixth arch (recurrent laryngeal nerve, CN X) forms the intrinsic laryngeal muscles (except the cricothyroid).'),
('fb26c707-fe5e-5a97-b84e-b8b6a13e81dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The thyroglossal duct:', '["The vitelline duct and its associated anatomical structures","The notochord and its associated anatomical structures","The pharyngeal pouch and its associated anatomical structures","The embryologic connection between the thyroid gland and the foramen cecum of the tongue (normall","The branchial cleft and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The embryologic connection between the thyroid gland and the foramen cecum of the tongue (normall', 'Thyroglossal duct cysts (midline neck, move with swallowing and tongue protrusion) are the most common congenital neck mass.'),
('2db934c7-21e9-57a4-9077-b42e0dd55d61'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'A thyroglossal duct cyst moves upward with:', '["Tongue protrusion","Neck flexion","Swallowing","Jaw opening","Neck extension"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue protrusion', 'Thyroglossal duct cysts (Sistrunk procedure: excise the cyst, the tract, and the central part of the hyoid bone to prevent recurrence).'),
('700abfad-91a8-50c0-b404-580669571d3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Branchial cleft cysts:', '["Lymph node abscesses and its associated anatomical structures","Parotid cysts and its associated anatomical structures","Remnants of the second branchial cleft (along the anterior border of the SCM","Cystic hygromas and its associated anatomical structures","Thyroglossal duct cysts and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Remnants of the second branchial cleft (along the anterior border of the SCM', 'Branchial cleft cysts (second arch anomalies) present along the anterior border of the SCM. The tract passes between the ICA and ECA.'),
('89bb7355-4c7c-54cc-8c2d-5e3b47929e3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'A branchial fistula (complete):', '["A dermoid cyst and its associated anatomical structures","A patent tract from the skin of the neck to the palatine tonsil (opens at the anterior border of","A blind-ending sinus and its associated anatomical structures","A thyroglossal duct cyst and its associated anatomical structures","An abscess and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A patent tract from the skin of the neck to the palatine tonsil (opens at the anterior border of', 'A complete branchial fistula (second arch) opens externally at the SCM border and internally in the tonsillar fossa (requires complete excision).'),
('9538a54c-95aa-5607-a225-8f189aff5be5'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Cystic hygroma (lymphangioma):', '["A congenital lymphatic malformation in the posterior triangle of the neck (contains fluid-filled","A branchial cleft cyst and its associated anatomical structures","A vascular malformation and its associated anatomical structures","A thyroglossal duct cyst and its associated anatomical structures","A dermoid cyst and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital lymphatic malformation in the posterior triangle of the neck (contains fluid-filled', 'Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation) transilluminates and is associated with Turner syndrome and other chromosomal abnormalities.'),
('f910b585-396c-5b76-9bd9-ac747c9ad690'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The lymph nodes of the neck are divided into:', '["Three levels: superficial, deep, and mediastinal","Lateral and medial groups","Levels I-VII","Five levels only","Two groups: superficial and deep"]'::jsonb, 'Levels I-VII', 'The neck lymph node levels (Robbins classification) guide the staging and treatment of head and neck cancer (levels I-VI in the neck, VII in the mediastinum).'),
('de4be67c-d515-5f3b-b307-7e946eea0f40'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Level I (submental and submandibular) nodes:', '["Drain the scalp and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the thyroid and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the nasopharynx and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the parotid and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the floor of the mouth, the anterior tongue, and the lower lip"]'::jsonb, 'Drain the floor of the mouth, the anterior tongue, and the lower lip', 'Level I is divided into Ia (submental, between the anterior bellies of the digastric) and Ib (submandibular, within the submandibular triangle).'),
('6ec925f1-6c2c-56fc-949d-63bb23d21aa4'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Level II (upper jugular) nodes:', '["In the posterior triangle and its associated anatomical structures","Along the middle third of the IJV and its associated anatomical structures","Nodes along the upper third of the IJV (from the skull base to the hyoid bone","Along the lower third and its associated anatomical structures","In the submandibular triangle and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Nodes along the upper third of the IJV (from the skull base to the hyoid bone', 'Level II nodes are the most common level involved in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma.'),
('cc876a3d-2aec-5cdb-ba4e-6d874ed987c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Level III (middle jugular) nodes:', '["Anterior triangle nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Posterior triangle nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Nodes along the middle third of the IJV (from the hyoid to the cricoid) — drain the larynx and th","Upper jugular nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Lower jugular nodes and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Nodes along the middle third of the IJV (from the hyoid to the cricoid) — drain the larynx and th', 'Level III nodes are at the level of the common carotid bifurcation (accessible for fine-needle aspiration).'),
('7137c010-48b8-5722-adb6-428a571417e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Level IV (lower jugular) nodes:', '["Anterior triangle and its associated anatomical structures","Nodes along the lower third of the IJV (from the cricoid to the clavicle) — drain the thyroid","Upper jugular and its associated anatomical structures","Posterior triangle and its associated anatomical structures","Middle jugular and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Nodes along the lower third of the IJV (from the cricoid to the clavicle) — drain the thyroid', 'Level IV nodes are at the base of the neck (adjacent to the thoracic duct on the left and the right lymphatic duct on the right).'),
('ae5f8a4c-bc84-51a4-8608-4523a1bc3591'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Level V (posterior triangle) nodes:', '["Jugular nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Nodes along the spinal accessory nerve and the transverse cervical vessels (drain the scalp","Supraclavicular nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Submandibular nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Anterior triangle nodes and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Nodes along the spinal accessory nerve and the transverse cervical vessels (drain the scalp', 'Level V nodes (posterior triangle) are commonly involved in nasopharyngeal carcinoma and lymphoma.'),
('b4c9e1e4-ee34-5bab-9d20-d7837c5e365a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Level VI (anterior compartment) nodes:', '["Mediastinal nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Supraclavicular nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Nodes in the central neck (pretracheal, paratracheal, precricoid/delphian) — drain the thyroid","Jugular nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Posterior triangle nodes and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Nodes in the central neck (pretracheal, paratracheal, precricoid/delphian) — drain the thyroid', 'Level VI nodes (the central compartment) are the first echelon of lymphatic drainage for thyroid cancer (must be dissected in thyroid cancer surgery).'),
('9b95e75f-dde2-559e-9d01-5840cf4f87a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The Delphian node (precricoid node):', '["A submandibular node","A posterior triangle node","A node in front of the cricoid cartilage","A parotid node","A jugular node"]'::jsonb, 'A node in front of the cricoid cartilage', 'The Delphian node (precricoid) is so named because it ''oracles'' the presence of thyroid or laryngeal cancer.'),
('df755b0c-1381-53bb-b60d-a910e06be91e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The sentinel lymph node:', '["The deepest node and its associated anatomical structures","The most superficial node and its associated anatomical structures","The first node in a regional lymphatic basin that drains a primary tumor (used for staging in mel","The largest node in the region and its associated anatomical structures","The most medial node and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The first node in a regional lymphatic basin that drains a primary tumor (used for staging in mel', 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB) avoids unnecessary lymph node dissection in clinically node-negative patients.'),
('7642b83b-a927-58e4-a898-0d636a9dba57'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The parotid lymph nodes drain:', '["The orbit and its associated anatomical structures","The nose and its associated anatomical structures","The pharynx and its associated anatomical structures","The lips and its associated anatomical structures","The skin of the forehead, temple, and the external ear"]'::jsonb, 'The skin of the forehead, temple, and the external ear', 'The parotid nodes (intraparotid and periparotid) are involved in parotid tumors (adenoid cystic carcinoma, mucoepidermoid carcinoma).'),
('a9619a01-349e-5ad8-ba59-8cc043f5cd75'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The retropharyngeal lymph nodes:', '["Nodes in the retropharyngeal space (drain the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and the paranasal sinuses","Drain the thyroid and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the larynx and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the trachea and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the esophagus and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Nodes in the retropharyngeal space (drain the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and the paranasal sinuses', 'Retropharyngeal nodes (medial and lateral groups) are not palpable on clinical examination (require imaging for assessment).'),
('5c6b5625-cb43-548d-8db8-229f3f8cbbbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The occipital lymph nodes:', '["Drain the posterior scalp and the upper neck","Drain the anterior neck","Drain the parotid","Drain the face","Drain the submandibular region"]'::jsonb, 'Drain the posterior scalp and the upper neck', 'The occipital nodes (suboccipital) are located at the apex of the posterior triangle at the base of the skull.'),
('d0f10331-45bb-5f92-b1ca-4b8fd68b2baf'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The postauricular (mastoid) lymph nodes:', '["Drain the submental region and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the anterior neck and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the parotid and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the nose and its associated anatomical structures","Drain the posterior auricular region and the external auditory canal"]'::jsonb, 'Drain the posterior auricular region and the external auditory canal', 'The postauricular nodes (behind the ear) are commonly enlarged in rubella and scalp infections.'),
('a68e21e7-4d15-50bb-af55-14e2b2a8441e'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The anterior cervical lymph nodes:', '["Deep cervical nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Submental nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Superficial nodes along the anterior jugular vein (drain the skin of the anterior neck into the d","Posterior cervical nodes and its associated anatomical structures","Submandibular nodes and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial nodes along the anterior jugular vein (drain the skin of the anterior neck into the d', 'The anterior cervical nodes (superficial) are often palpable in children and may be reactive in upper respiratory infections.'),
('2d2c8d6a-ff02-5c75-9e9c-c3d5faefe649'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The deep cervical lymph nodes:', '["The right lymphatic duct and its associated anatomical structures","The thoracic duct and its associated anatomical structures","Superficial cervical nodes and its associated anatomical structures","The major lymph node chain of the neck (along the IJV, receiving lymph from all other cervical no","The jugular trunk and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The major lymph node chain of the neck (along the IJV, receiving lymph from all other cervical no', 'The deep cervical nodes (jugular chain) consist of the superior (level II), middle (level III), and inferior (level IV) groups.'),
('3c7214e1-91fa-58f4-af69-7a9e7b4917f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The jugular lymphatic trunk:', '["The right lymphatic duct and its associated anatomical structures","The thoracic duct and its associated anatomical structures","The subclavian lymphatic trunk and its associated anatomical structures","The efferent lymphatic vessel from the deep cervical nodes (drains into the thoracic duct on the","The IJV and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'The efferent lymphatic vessel from the deep cervical nodes (drains into the thoracic duct on the', 'The jugular trunk (from the deep cervical nodes) drains directly into the venous system at the venous angle.'),
('581515b8-8fcd-5ae0-a4a5-26f8958b200a'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The cervical lymphadenopathy in adults is most commonly due to:', '["Reactive hyperplasia (infection), tuberculosis, lymphoma, or metastatic carcinoma (head and neck","Autoimmune disease and its associated anatomical structures","Sarcoidosis and its associated anatomical structures","Drug reaction and its associated anatomical structures","Kawasaki disease and its associated anatomical structures"]'::jsonb, 'Reactive hyperplasia (infection), tuberculosis, lymphoma, or metastatic carcinoma (head and neck', 'Unexplained cervical lymphadenopathy (persistent >2-4 weeks, firm, non-tender, matted) requires further evaluation (US, FNA, or excision biopsy).'),
('86cf6995-f9e3-52d9-a757-50f632dfb095'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) results from interruption of:', '["Facial nerve","Oculomotor nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Cervical sympathetic chain","Hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical sympathetic chain', 'Horner syndrome: interruption of sympathetic fibers anywhere from hypothalamus to C8-T2 (ciliospinal center of Budge) along carotid artery. Causes: Pancoast tumor, trauma, neck dissection.'),
('00d84966-27ad-5caa-9d99-3233106d0947'::uuid, 'Anatomy (Paper 2)', 'Head and Neck', 'The most common salivary gland tumor is:', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Warthin tumor","Acinic cell carcinoma","Adenoid cystic carcinoma","Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pleomorphic adenoma', 'Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common salivary gland tumor (60-70%). Typically in superficial parotid lobe. Mixed histology (epithelial + myoepithelial).');
